00:22:51.001 --> 00:22:54.250
Now, one could ask,
"Why did you not

00:22:54.251 --> 00:22:57.140
like the word Edge
anymore after few years?"

00:22:57.141 --> 00:22:58.330
I say, "Well, not really.

00:22:58.331 --> 00:23:00.350
I don't particular care

00:23:00.351 --> 00:23:03.990
about English word,"
and I know that for

00:23:03.991 --> 00:23:06.205
example Victor thinks that Fog is

00:23:06.206 --> 00:23:08.080
a very vague foggy word

00:23:08.081 --> 00:23:10.560
and why don't we just
keep using the word Edge.

00:23:10.561 --> 00:23:16.150
But, if there is an industry
including Microsoft, Cisco,

00:23:16.151 --> 00:23:18.190
Intel and quite a few others

00:23:18.191 --> 00:23:19.830
want to get together use a term,

00:23:19.831 --> 00:23:22.000
I say, "That's fine with me too."

00:23:22.001 --> 00:23:26.920
Decomposing functions along
this C-2-Things Continuum,

00:23:26.921 --> 00:23:29.785
Cloud-2-Things Continuum.

00:23:29.786 --> 00:23:34.740
So, this is sort of wrong
picture to show but let me

00:23:34.741 --> 00:23:39.510
first tell you what is perhaps
useful about it first.

00:23:39.511 --> 00:23:41.910
Now, on the one side
you've got the Cloud,

00:23:41.911 --> 00:23:43.720
on this other side you got,

00:23:43.721 --> 00:23:47.095
that looks like a fog or mist
or whatever you call it.

00:23:47.096 --> 00:23:50.915
Now, the interesting point
is that you need both.

00:23:50.916 --> 00:23:55.230
Now, Peter Levin's
provocative essay

00:23:55.231 --> 00:23:56.945
to say the death of Cloud,

00:23:56.946 --> 00:23:59.990
might be exaggerating
to make a point.

00:23:59.991 --> 00:24:02.990
I don't see the death of Cloud,

00:24:02.991 --> 00:24:05.160
I see the rise of the Edge and

00:24:05.161 --> 00:24:07.990
Fog and depending on
what you want to do.

00:24:07.991 --> 00:24:10.650
What belongs to Caesar
goes to Caesar,

00:24:10.651 --> 00:24:13.745
what belongs to God goes to God.

00:24:13.746 --> 00:24:17.160
Now, this picture is
however also wrong

00:24:17.161 --> 00:24:19.845
because it is not a dichotomy,

00:24:19.846 --> 00:24:21.390
it is a continuum.

00:24:21.391 --> 00:24:23.880
There is no digital sort
of boundary to say,

00:24:23.881 --> 00:24:30.975
"Yes," it's a choice
between either and or.

00:24:30.976 --> 00:24:34.995
Furthermore, the pendulum
swings back and forth.

00:24:34.996 --> 00:24:38.915
The time scale is
about 15-20 years or so.

00:24:38.916 --> 00:24:42.630
So, some argue that there's
nothing new under the sun

00:24:42.631 --> 00:24:44.300
then they were merrily swinging

00:24:44.301 --> 00:24:46.775
the pendulum every
two decades or so.

00:24:46.776 --> 00:24:49.560
Whether this NSF, or industry,

00:24:49.561 --> 00:24:53.880
or a PhD thesis and perhaps
it is not that cynical.

00:24:53.881 --> 00:24:55.340
I think every swing brings

00:24:55.341 --> 00:24:59.290
new ideas driven by
new context too.

00:24:59.291 --> 00:25:02.270
But, it is true that the rise

00:25:02.271 --> 00:25:05.320
of the Cloud and then now there's

00:25:05.321 --> 00:25:08.220
the rise of the Edge and Fog

00:25:08.221 --> 00:25:11.390
then big believer just
like Victor myself.

00:25:11.391 --> 00:25:12.950
But I won't be surprised that

00:25:12.951 --> 00:25:16.560
10 50 years later at
Microsoft Faculty Summit,

00:25:16.561 --> 00:25:21.075
whatever 2034, people will say,

00:25:21.076 --> 00:25:25.900
"Oh, this Edge thing
is just so like 2028."

00:25:25.901 --> 00:25:27.870
We should go centralization,

00:25:27.871 --> 00:25:29.330
we should look at economy of

00:25:29.331 --> 00:25:31.050
scale and somebody in
the audience will say,

00:25:31.051 --> 00:25:32.340
"I vaguely remember when I

00:25:32.341 --> 00:25:34.435
was young there's something
called the Cloud."

00:25:34.436 --> 00:25:39.215
So, this pendulum will keep
swinging and that is fine.

00:25:39.216 --> 00:25:42.730
There are many topics as Victor

00:25:42.731 --> 00:25:45.620
is saying one could
talk about it in Edge,

00:25:45.621 --> 00:25:48.680
Intelligence Edge
or Fog including

00:25:48.681 --> 00:25:51.920
I think the economic side
of it is very important.

00:25:51.921 --> 00:25:53.655
How do you incentivize?

00:25:53.656 --> 00:25:55.220
The security privacy is

00:25:55.221 --> 00:25:58.460
important but let
me just focus next

00:25:58.461 --> 00:26:00.650
10 minutes particularly on

00:26:00.651 --> 00:26:04.600
the Dispersive AI as one of the,

00:26:04.601 --> 00:26:06.090
in my mind, three main

00:26:06.091 --> 00:26:08.335
pillars software
application verticals.

00:26:08.336 --> 00:26:10.035
So, here you again,

00:26:10.036 --> 00:26:11.760
want to leverage
the fact that you

00:26:11.761 --> 00:26:13.860
got latency on your side.

00:26:13.861 --> 00:26:15.510
Latency in two ways.

00:26:15.511 --> 00:26:19.785
First, you can do Edge analytics
or stream or mining with

00:26:19.786 --> 00:26:23.120
the live streaming
of data coming to

00:26:23.121 --> 00:26:27.500
you and you can understand
it right here right now.

00:26:27.501 --> 00:26:29.550
The other is you dare to put in

00:26:29.551 --> 00:26:31.990
some feedback control loop
because you know

00:26:31.991 --> 00:26:35.020
that latency and
perhaps more importantly jitter

00:26:35.021 --> 00:26:36.740
is much more under control.

00:26:36.741 --> 00:26:40.000
Is small and almost
deterministic latency so

00:26:40.001 --> 00:26:43.925
you can run control loops
in cyber-physical systems.

00:26:43.926 --> 00:26:45.940
Now, you got to decompose

00:26:45.941 --> 00:26:47.780
the machine-learning computation

00:26:47.781 --> 00:26:51.210
and control into
disparate components and

00:26:51.211 --> 00:26:55.405
then they will be working
either collectively or

00:26:55.406 --> 00:26:58.120
jointly and you can think

00:26:58.121 --> 00:27:01.405
about pre-positioning and so on.

00:27:01.406 --> 00:27:04.100
So, this is about a year ago,

00:27:04.101 --> 00:27:05.885
we did a demo of

00:27:05.886 --> 00:27:10.650
one particular platform
that we did at Princeton.

00:27:10.651 --> 00:27:16.465
Think of this as
a MapReduce for Edge or Fog

00:27:16.466 --> 00:27:18.735
and what you want to do is

00:27:18.736 --> 00:27:23.075
to look at
opportunities to frame.

00:27:23.076 --> 00:27:24.990
How would you decompose

00:27:24.991 --> 00:27:27.680
machine-learning
computations in order

00:27:27.681 --> 00:27:29.890
to map to this new substrate?

00:27:29.891 --> 00:27:32.610
So, I'm going to fly
through some slides here

00:27:32.611 --> 00:27:35.600
but the typical chart
you want to see,

00:27:35.601 --> 00:27:37.635
this is just a toy example,

00:27:37.636 --> 00:27:40.195
last year is to say, "Well,

00:27:40.196 --> 00:27:43.760
between one extreme end
completely on the device,

00:27:43.761 --> 00:27:46.554
the other extreme end
completely on the Cloud,

00:27:46.555 --> 00:27:48.530
what happens in between?"

00:27:48.531 --> 00:27:50.400
While there's a trade-off between

00:27:50.401 --> 00:27:52.010
what's done locally and what

00:27:52.011 --> 00:27:55.625
is coordinated
communicated globally,

00:27:55.626 --> 00:27:59.465
you want this summation
to be smaller than

00:27:59.466 --> 00:28:01.870
either end and that's

00:28:01.871 --> 00:28:06.140
a little comparison between
what we did in Spark.

00:28:06.650 --> 00:28:09.625
The unique feature
really is that,

00:28:09.626 --> 00:28:14.640
you got an Edge or Fog to size.

00:28:14.641 --> 00:28:17.350
On the challenging side,

00:28:17.351 --> 00:28:20.945
there's heterogeneity
unlike the Cloud,

00:28:20.946 --> 00:28:25.270
you don't have racks
of servers that run on

00:28:25.271 --> 00:28:30.120
the same OS and similar
hardware capabilities.

00:28:30.121 --> 00:28:33.905
You've got variability
even volatility

00:28:33.906 --> 00:28:36.665
because unlike a rack of servers,

00:28:36.666 --> 00:28:38.250
these little things they

00:28:38.251 --> 00:28:41.220
move perhaps and they
may not always be

00:28:41.221 --> 00:28:43.990
available and you got constraints

00:28:43.991 --> 00:28:48.315
bandwidth for sure and maybe
even battery constraints.

00:28:48.316 --> 00:28:50.070
But on the other hand,

00:28:50.071 --> 00:28:53.000
you also got the nice things such

00:28:53.001 --> 00:28:58.695
as physical proximity to
sensors and actuators.

00:28:58.696 --> 00:29:02.090
So, the challenge is to
take care of heterogeneity,

00:29:02.091 --> 00:29:04.830
variability and
constraints and take

00:29:04.831 --> 00:29:08.130
advantage of the proximity.

00:29:08.131 --> 00:29:12.260
Now, how would you decompose
a job whether it's

00:29:12.261 --> 00:29:14.010
counting words in a file

00:29:14.011 --> 00:29:17.120
or slightly more
interesting let's say,

00:29:17.121 --> 00:29:20.465
run a least squares for example.

00:29:20.466 --> 00:29:23.045
One of the simplest things
you can think of.

00:29:23.046 --> 00:29:25.620
If you run a large-scale
least squares

00:29:25.621 --> 00:29:27.475
as embedded in the loop

00:29:27.476 --> 00:29:31.880
of some particular optimization
for machine learning,

00:29:31.881 --> 00:29:34.560
you know how to do
that by decomposing

00:29:34.561 --> 00:29:37.470
the job into parallel
computation on a rack of

00:29:37.471 --> 00:29:41.394
servers very well but
how would you decompose

00:29:41.395 --> 00:29:46.090
that job over a very
different substrate?

00:29:46.091 --> 00:29:48.090
The add Fox substrate.

00:29:48.091 --> 00:29:51.750
So, here are two toy
examples to illustrate

00:29:51.751 --> 00:29:53.885
this notion of decomposing

00:29:53.886 --> 00:29:57.010
the computation over
this kind of substrate.

00:29:57.011 --> 00:29:59.845
One is about Drone Camera work

00:29:59.846 --> 00:30:04.390
with Aakanksha and
Shirley in my lab.

00:30:04.960 --> 00:30:09.325
So, imagine now in this
particular application topic

00:30:09.326 --> 00:30:12.144
you've got the outdoor,

00:30:12.145 --> 00:30:17.045
say a sports event like
the FIFA World Cup soccer,

00:30:17.046 --> 00:30:20.510
and people have thought about

00:30:20.511 --> 00:30:24.620
using cameras over
cross wires for example,

00:30:24.621 --> 00:30:27.380
but if you use drones to

00:30:27.381 --> 00:30:31.400
capture and to transmit,
what would happen?

00:30:32.690 --> 00:30:35.960
So, here is one way to

00:30:35.961 --> 00:30:38.745
look at the choices
are of architecture.

00:30:38.746 --> 00:30:41.940
On the one end, you can
make everything locally

00:30:41.941 --> 00:30:47.110
done but then you lose
the collaboration.

00:30:47.111 --> 00:30:48.340
You may miss some of

00:30:48.341 --> 00:30:50.790
the important scenes in the
coverage and you may have to

00:30:50.791 --> 00:30:56.090
reduce the video transmission
right out of those drones.

00:30:56.091 --> 00:30:57.130
On the other hand,

00:30:57.131 --> 00:30:59.690
you have a fully
centralized version of it

00:30:59.691 --> 00:31:04.390
and you have to make
decisions centrally.

00:31:04.391 --> 00:31:07.585
Now, this could be

00:31:07.586 --> 00:31:10.300
the Azure Cloud way out there or

00:31:10.301 --> 00:31:13.225
it could be even a big
server on the ground.

00:31:13.226 --> 00:31:14.675
That big server to

00:31:14.676 --> 00:31:17.785
all the little drones
feels like the Cloud.

00:31:17.786 --> 00:31:21.100
It's just like a little rock

00:31:21.101 --> 00:31:24.430
to us may feel like
a mountain to the ends.

00:31:24.431 --> 00:31:28.100
But you're going to suffer
the round-trip time limits.

00:31:28.101 --> 00:31:30.400
So, the ability to coordinate in

00:31:30.401 --> 00:31:34.115
real time in response to
game dynamics will be limited.

00:31:34.116 --> 00:31:35.770
So, here is one

00:31:35.771 --> 00:31:38.050
particular decomposition,
I'm not arguing,

00:31:38.051 --> 00:31:40.245
this is the best
the decomposition

00:31:40.246 --> 00:31:42.460
even for this Application,

00:31:42.461 --> 00:31:43.880
but you see that the drones

00:31:43.881 --> 00:31:46.530
form little Fog network
among themselves.

00:31:46.531 --> 00:31:49.340
They would take care of
the following functions;

00:31:49.341 --> 00:31:52.030
trajectory planning and
collision avoidance,

00:31:52.031 --> 00:31:56.410
and then there is
a little central coordinator for

00:31:56.411 --> 00:31:58.950
next target location optimisation

00:31:58.951 --> 00:32:01.535
and deciding which drone
should be doing what.

00:32:01.536 --> 00:32:02.790
So, you've got eight drones,

00:32:02.791 --> 00:32:04.210
let's say, up in the air.

00:32:04.211 --> 00:32:07.640
Which of those should help you to

00:32:07.641 --> 00:32:10.800
cover the scenes and which should

00:32:10.801 --> 00:32:13.735
help you to relay
the video streams?

00:32:13.736 --> 00:32:17.625
So, that will be done
with coordination.

00:32:17.626 --> 00:32:21.300
So, there is
a Steel Controller up

00:32:21.301 --> 00:32:23.650
there and then you've got

00:32:23.651 --> 00:32:26.679
the sensors on the boss
and the players,

00:32:26.680 --> 00:32:28.130
and then you got the, let's say,

00:32:28.131 --> 00:32:31.495
eight or whatever number
of drones up in the air.

00:32:31.496 --> 00:32:33.205
So the drones now,

00:32:33.206 --> 00:32:35.275
they got to decide to cover,

00:32:35.276 --> 00:32:36.995
they got to decide to transmit,

00:32:36.996 --> 00:32:38.705
and you can do
other fancy things,

00:32:38.706 --> 00:32:40.405
3D rendering and so on,

00:32:40.406 --> 00:32:42.780
if you want to do
that in real time.

00:32:42.781 --> 00:32:45.990
There is actually a trade-off
between maximizing

00:32:45.991 --> 00:32:49.220
Coverage and
maximizing Throughput,

00:32:49.221 --> 00:32:51.835
and you only got
that many drones.

00:32:51.836 --> 00:32:53.814
So for each drone,

00:32:53.815 --> 00:32:57.450
should that drone
help with Coverage or

00:32:57.451 --> 00:33:01.435
should that help with relaying
and enhancing Throughput.

00:33:01.436 --> 00:33:02.890
It turns out you can run a

00:33:02.891 --> 00:33:06.070
integer programming
joint optimization

00:33:06.071 --> 00:33:08.000
and there are
some heuristics will allow

00:33:08.001 --> 00:33:10.005
you to solve it very fast,

00:33:10.006 --> 00:33:13.050
even though not truly optimally.

00:33:13.850 --> 00:33:17.690
Just a little chart
to show what we did,

00:33:17.691 --> 00:33:21.300
I guess illegally on the field on

00:33:21.301 --> 00:33:26.395
Sunday mornings near Princeton,
I shouldn't say that.

00:33:26.396 --> 00:33:28.410
Probably legally,

00:33:28.411 --> 00:33:31.585
flying the drones and

00:33:31.586 --> 00:33:33.885
you look at the
trade-off over there.

00:33:33.886 --> 00:33:35.690
In this particular case,

00:33:35.691 --> 00:33:37.570
it turns out that if
you've got eight drones,

00:33:37.571 --> 00:33:39.610
follow them to focus on coverage,

00:33:39.611 --> 00:33:41.045
follow them to focus on,

00:33:41.046 --> 00:33:42.230
streaming is the best.

00:33:42.231 --> 00:33:43.660
Now which is Covering?

00:33:43.661 --> 00:33:44.905
Which is streaming?

00:33:44.906 --> 00:33:47.925
That depends on
the real time situation

00:33:47.926 --> 00:33:52.135
that is actually coordinated
by the Cloud, if it will.

00:33:52.136 --> 00:33:55.370
So, the message here is not
about the Application per se.

00:33:55.371 --> 00:33:58.290
We can go into that detail
on another day,

00:33:58.291 --> 00:34:00.775
but to say if you
want to coordinate

00:34:00.776 --> 00:34:03.915
group of drones working
with each other,

00:34:03.916 --> 00:34:05.840
certain functions can be done

00:34:05.841 --> 00:34:08.425
by themselves on the drones and

00:34:08.426 --> 00:34:14.290
others should be done on
the ground with a little server.

00:34:14.291 --> 00:34:17.915
So, the second
quick illustrative example

00:34:17.916 --> 00:34:22.650
is something that we were
doing it last month,

00:34:22.651 --> 00:34:23.875
it's still ongoing, it's

00:34:23.876 --> 00:34:26.685
some preliminary results
here to share.

00:34:26.686 --> 00:34:28.895
It's about ARVR and

00:34:28.896 --> 00:34:33.155
particularly the technical
problem of Object placement,

00:34:33.156 --> 00:34:36.385
so that they don't
block important things,

00:34:36.386 --> 00:34:38.995
and don't confuse the viewer,

00:34:38.996 --> 00:34:43.865
and we want to personalize
the placement challenge.

00:34:43.866 --> 00:34:45.580
The question is, where do you

00:34:45.581 --> 00:34:47.735
learn about the
personal preferences?

00:34:47.736 --> 00:34:50.315
And where does
the learning happen?

00:34:50.316 --> 00:34:52.320
By the way, that is actually

00:34:52.321 --> 00:34:55.570
the Princeton Edge lab outside in

00:34:55.571 --> 00:34:57.735
the corridor over
there and those are

00:34:57.736 --> 00:35:01.450
the holograms in that experiment.

00:35:01.451 --> 00:35:04.845
So again, the main message
is that there's

00:35:04.846 --> 00:35:09.285
a variety of choices in
network decomposition here.

00:35:09.286 --> 00:35:11.825
During the policy learning phase,

00:35:11.826 --> 00:35:14.020
there is a user,
there's an agent,

00:35:14.021 --> 00:35:17.880
and the agent we're trying
to mimic the user and

00:35:17.881 --> 00:35:20.505
learn what is
the personal preference

00:35:20.506 --> 00:35:22.560
of overlapping and a foreground,

00:35:22.561 --> 00:35:24.725
background placement
of the holograms.

00:35:24.726 --> 00:35:26.240
Then there is, secondly,

00:35:26.241 --> 00:35:28.300
a policy execution phase

00:35:28.301 --> 00:35:32.310
after you've gone through
the learning phase.

00:35:32.311 --> 00:35:35.260
In both phases you
can either have

00:35:35.261 --> 00:35:38.370
a little server there or you
can have the end device,

00:35:38.371 --> 00:35:44.115
in this case, the AR set
to do the computation.

00:35:44.116 --> 00:35:47.180
What we found in
this particular case is

00:35:47.181 --> 00:35:50.960
a trade-off whereby the policy is

00:35:50.961 --> 00:35:54.990
learned with the
computation primarily on

00:35:54.991 --> 00:35:59.060
the little server next
to these AR devices,

00:35:59.061 --> 00:36:00.755
but then in execution,

00:36:00.756 --> 00:36:02.830
the parameter choices of

00:36:02.831 --> 00:36:05.680
the learned policy is
done in real time.

00:36:05.681 --> 00:36:07.970
That's not again surprising

00:36:07.971 --> 00:36:11.310
trade-off but is one that worked,

00:36:11.311 --> 00:36:18.140
and this is a quick video,

00:36:18.141 --> 00:36:21.035
it's one minute, I'll probably
skip in a few seconds.

00:36:21.036 --> 00:36:24.290
On the left-hand side
is the user,

00:36:24.291 --> 00:36:26.055
on the right-hand side
is the agent.

00:36:26.056 --> 00:36:28.015
This is during
the learning phase.

00:36:28.016 --> 00:36:29.920
You want to learn
the policy before

00:36:29.921 --> 00:36:33.055
you execute the
policy in real time

00:36:33.056 --> 00:36:35.000
and it shows how

00:36:35.001 --> 00:36:39.140
the right-hand side agent is
learning quite efficiently,

00:36:39.141 --> 00:36:41.550
based on what the user is doing.

00:36:41.551 --> 00:36:42.910
To start it out,

00:36:42.911 --> 00:36:45.390
if you still remember,
15 seconds ago.

00:36:45.391 --> 00:36:49.370
Way off, whether
the hologram should be

00:36:49.371 --> 00:36:52.510
placed in covering
the real object

00:36:52.511 --> 00:36:53.860
or behind the real object,

00:36:53.861 --> 00:36:59.100
or where it should be but
within 10-15 iterations,

00:36:59.101 --> 00:37:01.190
it learned very fast.

00:37:01.191 --> 00:37:07.940
Let me just skip to
the next slide there.

00:37:12.840 --> 00:37:15.460
So, one way to visualize that

00:37:15.461 --> 00:37:19.320
the learning processes is
after a few demos, basically,

00:37:19.321 --> 00:37:23.440
all those little dots
are the decisions

00:37:23.441 --> 00:37:25.095
by the agent to learning

00:37:25.096 --> 00:37:27.605
the user preference and
personalized placement,

00:37:27.606 --> 00:37:30.845
and that they all aggregate
to the correct place.

00:37:30.846 --> 00:37:33.480
Now, what is
interesting is that if

00:37:33.481 --> 00:37:37.645
you don't use
State-Space reduction,

00:37:37.646 --> 00:37:40.595
then even after many more rounds,

00:37:40.596 --> 00:37:44.405
you still are all
over the places.

00:37:44.406 --> 00:37:45.860
On the left-hand side is

00:37:45.861 --> 00:37:47.710
you do use State-Space reduction,

00:37:47.711 --> 00:37:49.350
right hand side is you do use

00:37:49.351 --> 00:37:52.285
it and you can clearly
see the benefits.

00:37:52.286 --> 00:37:54.185
Now, the State-Space reduction is

00:37:54.186 --> 00:37:56.300
carried out not on the AR

00:37:56.301 --> 00:38:00.825
themselves but on
the little server.

00:38:00.826 --> 00:38:02.860
So again, the main message there

00:38:02.861 --> 00:38:04.905
is not it about AR placement,

00:38:04.906 --> 00:38:07.340
personalisation is that there's

00:38:07.341 --> 00:38:11.720
a choice of where do you
carry out the learning?

00:38:11.960 --> 00:38:15.200
One possibility is let the policy

00:38:15.201 --> 00:38:17.840
stay in the Cloud, if you will,

00:38:17.841 --> 00:38:20.550
and let the actual parameter

00:38:20.551 --> 00:38:23.310
and execution stay
on the end devices.

00:38:23.311 --> 00:38:27.419
So, the overall question
really is, where,

00:38:27.420 --> 00:38:29.895
as in the horizontal
decomposition,

00:38:29.896 --> 00:38:32.460
where do you place a function?

00:38:32.461 --> 00:38:33.710
We haven't even got to

00:38:33.711 --> 00:38:36.345
the point of where do
you place the data?

00:38:36.346 --> 00:38:41.415
What is the interface among
these modules over there?

00:38:41.416 --> 00:38:43.260
So, my last slide is just to

00:38:43.261 --> 00:38:45.210
highlight what is,
perhaps obvious,

00:38:45.211 --> 00:38:50.470
on why is it even interesting
to explore that today?

00:38:50.471 --> 00:38:53.030
I think of the acronym scale.

00:38:53.031 --> 00:38:54.860
I learned that if
you want to write

00:38:54.861 --> 00:38:56.690
a winning NSF
proposal you have to

00:38:56.691 --> 00:38:59.105
come up with good acronym.

00:38:59.106 --> 00:39:04.400
Although this was refuted by
Jim Crosby last night to me,

00:39:04.401 --> 00:39:07.430
that it is not
a necessary condition.

00:39:07.431 --> 00:39:09.040
But I guess it doesn't hurt.

00:39:09.041 --> 00:39:12.390
So, S is the potential
advantage on security.

00:39:12.391 --> 00:39:14.680
The less distance it traverses,

00:39:14.681 --> 00:39:17.330
more privacy is
potentially protected.

00:39:17.331 --> 00:39:19.810
One could argue the
other way, to say, "Wow,

00:39:19.811 --> 00:39:21.685
these little things
trusting them with

00:39:21.686 --> 00:39:23.330
more and more functions but

00:39:23.331 --> 00:39:24.965
they are easier to hack into",

00:39:24.966 --> 00:39:27.580
and their update is
not done properly.

00:39:27.581 --> 00:39:29.920
Cognition is,
because you are much

00:39:29.921 --> 00:39:32.475
closer to the end
user and ambiance,

00:39:32.476 --> 00:39:34.755
you have a better understanding

00:39:34.756 --> 00:39:37.015
of user-centric objective.

00:39:37.016 --> 00:39:40.940
Agility just refers to the fact
that generally speaking,

00:39:40.941 --> 00:39:42.610
you go into the Cloud,

00:39:42.611 --> 00:39:45.060
the core Network of LT and so on.

00:39:45.061 --> 00:39:47.210
You go through 3GPP and it takes

00:39:47.211 --> 00:39:50.830
forever to update and change,

00:39:50.831 --> 00:39:53.875
whereas if you rely
on that end devices,

00:39:53.876 --> 00:39:57.835
the agile development
process is much faster.

00:39:57.836 --> 00:40:00.270
Latency, we mentioned, as
the number one reason,

00:40:00.271 --> 00:40:01.870
as Victor also highlighted that

00:40:01.871 --> 00:40:03.550
people think about why do I even

00:40:03.551 --> 00:40:07.215
care and need anything
other than the Cloud.

00:40:07.216 --> 00:40:09.570
Efficiency is
actually interesting.

00:40:09.571 --> 00:40:13.335
There's the D to D efficiency.

00:40:13.336 --> 00:40:15.845
Device talking to device,

00:40:15.846 --> 00:40:18.700
but then there's also
the device for device,

00:40:18.701 --> 00:40:20.565
D for D efficiency,

00:40:20.566 --> 00:40:24.080
that because you have
proximal distance,

00:40:24.081 --> 00:40:28.110
you can authorize the idle
resources connectivity,

00:40:28.111 --> 00:40:29.725
computation and so on

00:40:29.726 --> 00:40:32.320
that are in physical
proximity to each other.

00:40:32.321 --> 00:40:34.835
So, S-C-A-L-E these are

00:40:34.836 --> 00:40:37.970
the reasons I think of
why Network decomposition,

00:40:37.971 --> 00:40:43.120
horizontally closer to
the user's Edge/Fog

00:40:43.121 --> 00:40:46.915
is becoming ever so more
interesting. Thanks.

00:40:46.916 --> 00:40:53.140
&gt;&gt; Okay. So, we can ask
one question, if there's one.

00:40:53.141 --> 00:40:56.540
We'll have, hopefully, that
is sometimes after the panel.

00:40:56.541 --> 00:41:00.090
But in the mean time, somebody
wants to ask a question?

00:41:00.620 --> 00:41:05.850
&gt;&gt; You can ask the question
and I'll answer.

00:41:08.230 --> 00:41:11.225
&gt;&gt; I have a bunch of
questions for you.

00:41:11.226 --> 00:41:13.205
We'll do it when we do the panel.

00:41:13.206 --> 00:41:13.520
&gt;&gt; All right.

00:41:13.521 --> 00:41:17.110
&gt;&gt; Thank you. Yeah. So, great

00:41:17.111 --> 00:41:20.080
pleasure to introduce
Mark Kishore Ramachandran.

00:41:20.081 --> 00:41:22.680
He actually goes by
the name Kishore

00:41:22.681 --> 00:41:24.920
and then for those

00:41:24.921 --> 00:41:27.075
of you who are from India
know Kishore Kumar Kishore,

00:41:27.076 --> 00:41:29.630
anyway so, my jokes are so flat.

00:41:29.631 --> 00:41:31.710
Okay. So anyway, so he got

00:41:31.711 --> 00:41:34.420
his PhD from University
of Wisconsin, Madison.

00:41:34.421 --> 00:41:37.970
He is now a professor
at Georgia Tech.

00:41:37.971 --> 00:41:41.210
He's interests are in
parallel distributed systems

00:41:41.211 --> 00:41:43.820
and Edge, as you can see.

00:41:43.821 --> 00:41:46.470
He's been working on
this space for a while.

00:41:46.471 --> 00:41:49.330
He's quite passionate about

00:41:49.331 --> 00:41:52.810
providing connectivity and
services in the rural areas.

00:41:52.811 --> 00:41:56.820
I actually was there in one
of the NSF workshops he

00:41:56.821 --> 00:41:58.140
had actually organized with

00:41:58.141 --> 00:42:01.025
Aneesh, and quite fascinating.

00:42:01.026 --> 00:42:02.490
With that, please take it away.

00:42:02.491 --> 00:42:05.360
&gt;&gt; Well, I mean,
there is this debate

00:42:05.361 --> 00:42:11.925
that Victor started
with Edge versus Fog,

00:42:11.926 --> 00:42:13.090
but let me ask you a quiz,

00:42:13.091 --> 00:42:16.345
how many of you know
Victor's real first name?

00:42:16.346 --> 00:42:20.150
Any of you who knows?

00:42:20.151 --> 00:42:23.660
Among, what's
the name, do you know?

00:42:25.230 --> 00:42:33.440
Paramvir is real first name
and it means supreme hero.

00:42:34.920 --> 00:42:37.290
It sort of loose- and you should

00:42:37.291 --> 00:42:38.985
ask him during the panel.

00:42:38.986 --> 00:42:41.040
During the panel part
asked him how did

00:42:41.041 --> 00:42:43.875
he got the name, Victor.

00:42:43.876 --> 00:42:49.080
So, I think Mung and Victor,

00:42:49.081 --> 00:42:51.750
both of them gave a nice segue

00:42:51.751 --> 00:42:53.490
to what I'm going to talk about,

00:42:53.491 --> 00:42:55.845
and the title of my talk

00:42:55.846 --> 00:43:00.490
is Elevating the Edge to
be a Peer of the Cloud.

00:43:00.491 --> 00:43:04.660
So, that's the spiel that
I'm going to talk about.

00:43:05.210 --> 00:43:08.225
Of course I wouldn't be
standing here but for

00:43:08.226 --> 00:43:11.420
all my students who've
done all the work that

00:43:11.421 --> 00:43:13.490
I'm going to highlight

00:43:13.491 --> 00:43:16.780
a little bit in describing
what we're doing.

00:43:16.781 --> 00:43:18.845
So, I think IoT

00:43:18.846 --> 00:43:21.670
and the scale of applications
that run on the IoT,

00:43:21.671 --> 00:43:24.815
all of these are things that
you all are familiar with

00:43:24.816 --> 00:43:26.555
and the key thing that

00:43:26.556 --> 00:43:29.135
Mung and Victor
pointed out is that,

00:43:29.136 --> 00:43:33.040
future applications on
the IoT infrastructure

00:43:33.041 --> 00:43:34.480
is going to have
this characteristic of

00:43:34.481 --> 00:43:37.019
sensing, processing,
and actuating

00:43:37.020 --> 00:43:39.339
which means that
latency becomes really

00:43:39.340 --> 00:43:43.430
critical in going from
sensing to actuation.

00:43:43.431 --> 00:43:45.310
Cloud computing as we know it is

00:43:45.311 --> 00:43:47.250
great for throughput
oriented application,

00:43:47.251 --> 00:43:49.130
but not latency
sensitive applications.

00:43:49.131 --> 00:43:50.905
There are also
other considerations

00:43:50.906 --> 00:43:53.410
regulatory considerations
and backhaul bandwidth

00:43:53.411 --> 00:43:54.140
and so on.

00:43:54.141 --> 00:43:57.435
All of these may argue for moving

00:43:57.436 --> 00:44:01.630
the computations away
from the Cloud and

00:44:01.631 --> 00:44:04.020
that's where Fog/Edge Computing

00:44:04.021 --> 00:44:07.360
comes in and the rules as Mung

00:44:07.361 --> 00:44:10.055
pointed out it's
really a hierarchy of

00:44:10.056 --> 00:44:13.500
devices all the way from
the client devices,

00:44:13.501 --> 00:44:17.215
to routers, to servers,
all the way to the Cloud.

00:44:17.216 --> 00:44:22.610
So, that's the continuum
that exists in this world.

00:44:22.800 --> 00:44:27.610
Now, what I want to
emphasize is the fact that

00:44:27.611 --> 00:44:30.010
today Edge computing as

00:44:30.011 --> 00:44:33.445
we know it is a slave
of the Cloud.

00:44:33.446 --> 00:44:35.470
There are lots of very

00:44:35.471 --> 00:44:39.040
interesting platform
that had been designed,

00:44:39.041 --> 00:44:42.240
Arjmand is going to talk
about IoT Azure Edge,

00:44:42.241 --> 00:44:45.635
Cisco has its own IoX platform,

00:44:45.636 --> 00:44:48.210
but all of these require

00:44:48.211 --> 00:44:50.640
that the coordination is
coming from the Cloud

00:44:50.641 --> 00:44:53.160
and there was a good example

00:44:53.161 --> 00:44:56.380
that Mung already pointed
out in terms of drones,

00:44:56.381 --> 00:44:57.600
coordinating with one another.

00:44:57.601 --> 00:45:00.410
So, that's the idea
behind wanting

00:45:00.411 --> 00:45:03.755
to elevate the Edge
to be at the peer to

00:45:03.756 --> 00:45:06.810
the Cloud and today actually
we are beholden to the Cloud

00:45:06.811 --> 00:45:10.215
because if I want to send
a text message to Arjmand,

00:45:10.216 --> 00:45:12.985
I have to go to the Cloud
and come back to him, right?

00:45:12.986 --> 00:45:17.105
So, that just doesn't
seem right to us.

00:45:17.106 --> 00:45:18.750
So, vision for the future is

00:45:18.751 --> 00:45:21.115
that the Edge is going to become

00:45:21.116 --> 00:45:22.750
a peer of the Cloud and there's

00:45:22.751 --> 00:45:25.545
prior art that Victor
already pointed out,

00:45:25.546 --> 00:45:29.060
Cloudlets and MAUI and

00:45:29.061 --> 00:45:31.810
all of them are trying
to do the following,

00:45:31.811 --> 00:45:33.075
which is sort of take

00:45:33.076 --> 00:45:36.090
computations from the air
as a client devices,

00:45:36.091 --> 00:45:40.000
and launch it onto the
server that is nearby,

00:45:40.001 --> 00:45:41.925
but in the limit what
we want to do is,

00:45:41.926 --> 00:45:43.190
make the Edge autonomous,

00:45:43.191 --> 00:45:45.040
even if it is disconnected
from the Cloud.

00:45:45.041 --> 00:45:46.845
I can give lots of reasons

00:45:46.846 --> 00:45:49.700
why this might be
a good thing because the

00:45:49.701 --> 00:45:52.500
interacting entities if you
think self-connected you

00:45:52.501 --> 00:45:55.515
know connected vehicles
applications and so on,

00:45:55.516 --> 00:45:58.195
there is lot of things
that need to happen

00:45:58.196 --> 00:46:02.145
horizontally among
the Edge nodes.

00:46:02.146 --> 00:46:05.110
Even more importantly,
if you think

00:46:05.111 --> 00:46:07.220
about disaster recovery
and things like that,

00:46:07.221 --> 00:46:09.260
they may not even have kind
of connection to the Cloud,

00:46:09.261 --> 00:46:10.485
and in that case,

00:46:10.486 --> 00:46:12.625
the drone example was
a great one, right?

00:46:12.626 --> 00:46:14.640
So, if I have

00:46:14.641 --> 00:46:16.400
the ability to connect

00:46:16.401 --> 00:46:18.960
to even if I have
disconnections among them,

00:46:18.961 --> 00:46:21.165
if I have the ability
to connect to somebody,

00:46:21.166 --> 00:46:22.930
I can actually communicate and

00:46:22.931 --> 00:46:24.990
then eventually my information

00:46:24.991 --> 00:46:26.815
will get passed on to the others

00:46:26.816 --> 00:46:29.120
and we can have
a happy situation.

00:46:29.121 --> 00:46:31.990
So, these are motivating
reasons why you may want to

00:46:31.991 --> 00:46:36.490
elevate the Edge to be
a peer of the Cloud.

00:46:36.491 --> 00:46:40.025
But there are lots of
challenges making this happen.

00:46:40.026 --> 00:46:43.940
First of all, as the Cloud
as we know it today,

00:46:43.941 --> 00:46:45.815
is so powerful mainly because of

00:46:45.816 --> 00:46:47.945
all the infrastructure
that has gone into it.

00:46:47.946 --> 00:46:49.460
Right? There is a

00:46:49.461 --> 00:46:51.730
no programming models,
storage abstractions,

00:46:51.731 --> 00:46:55.335
pub/sub systems and so on
that make it a powerful

00:46:55.336 --> 00:47:01.380
development engine for rapidly
prototyping applications.

00:47:01.381 --> 00:47:06.550
Now we have geo-distributed
Edge nodes and we

00:47:06.551 --> 00:47:09.120
want to provide
the same sort of ability

00:47:09.121 --> 00:47:11.740
for rapidly prototyping
applications.

00:47:11.741 --> 00:47:13.425
And what that means is that,

00:47:13.426 --> 00:47:15.185
we need powerful frameworks,

00:47:15.186 --> 00:47:18.940
akin to the Cloud to be
available at the Edge.

00:47:18.941 --> 00:47:22.955
Yesterday Albert Greenberg was

00:47:22.956 --> 00:47:26.030
emphasizing the need for

00:47:26.031 --> 00:47:29.855
making sure that
recovery is important,

00:47:29.856 --> 00:47:32.470
which means that when you
have distributed data,

00:47:32.471 --> 00:47:34.170
then you have to worry
about replication

00:47:34.171 --> 00:47:35.890
in a geo-distributed fashion
so that you can

00:47:35.891 --> 00:47:38.940
avoid correlated failures that

00:47:38.941 --> 00:47:40.780
might happen in
a particular neighborhood.

00:47:40.781 --> 00:47:43.030
So, those are important
and when you do that,

00:47:43.031 --> 00:47:47.100
then there is the latency and
consistency trade-offs

00:47:47.101 --> 00:47:48.510
that comes about, right?

00:47:48.511 --> 00:47:51.420
So, those are things that
you have to worry about.

00:47:51.421 --> 00:47:53.945
As they are similar to the Cloud,

00:47:53.946 --> 00:47:58.315
we want the same sort
of elasticity of

00:47:58.316 --> 00:48:02.195
deploying application
components rapidly and

00:48:02.196 --> 00:48:05.870
the fact that you might have
the same IoT infrastructure,

00:48:05.871 --> 00:48:07.680
that is simultaneously
being used by

00:48:07.681 --> 00:48:10.000
multiple applications means that

00:48:10.001 --> 00:48:12.575
you have to have worry
about multi-tenancy.

00:48:12.576 --> 00:48:16.195
All of these are issues
that are challenging.

00:48:16.196 --> 00:48:18.570
In the remaining time,

00:48:18.571 --> 00:48:20.880
what I'm going to do is you
know lots of people are

00:48:20.881 --> 00:48:23.280
working on interesting
areas related to

00:48:23.281 --> 00:48:25.380
these topics and I'm
going to give you

00:48:25.381 --> 00:48:27.690
a little bit of a sort
of a bird's eye view

00:48:27.691 --> 00:48:30.215
into some of our own thoughts

00:48:30.216 --> 00:48:33.145
on addressing some
of these challenges.

00:48:33.146 --> 00:48:37.195
So, I'll talk about
a programming model

00:48:37.196 --> 00:48:41.960
that goes across the continuum
between- in some sense

00:48:41.961 --> 00:48:45.070
enabling application
similar to what Mung

00:48:45.071 --> 00:48:48.360
was talking about in
being able to share

00:48:48.361 --> 00:48:51.965
information across
the Edge as well as

00:48:51.966 --> 00:48:53.830
vertically with a Cloud

00:48:53.831 --> 00:48:57.115
and then how do you do
Geo-distributed data replication.

00:48:57.116 --> 00:49:00.305
Then also motivate
some example applications

00:49:00.306 --> 00:49:02.255
that might use autonomous Edge.

00:49:02.256 --> 00:49:04.750
So, that's the thing
that I want to do in

00:49:04.751 --> 00:49:07.330
the last in the next
10 minutes or so.

00:49:07.331 --> 00:49:08.630
But obviously I cannot do

00:49:08.631 --> 00:49:10.980
full justice for
that and I invite

00:49:10.981 --> 00:49:12.320
you to talk to me

00:49:12.321 --> 00:49:14.850
or look at some of these
publications that we have here.

00:49:14.851 --> 00:49:16.210
So, first let me talk about

00:49:16.211 --> 00:49:18.765
the Geo-distributed
programming model.

00:49:18.766 --> 00:49:20.000
The idea is that you

00:49:20.001 --> 00:49:23.210
have sensors that
might be generating

00:49:23.211 --> 00:49:25.610
data and the application
itself will be

00:49:25.611 --> 00:49:26.800
a dataflow graph that you can

00:49:26.801 --> 00:49:28.470
represent it as a dataflow graph,

00:49:28.471 --> 00:49:30.940
and what you wanna do is Victor

00:49:30.941 --> 00:49:33.560
talked about what
are the SLAs, right?

00:49:33.561 --> 00:49:36.565
SLAs in these kinds of
applications tend to be latency.

00:49:36.566 --> 00:49:38.880
So, given the latency
properties of

00:49:38.881 --> 00:49:41.850
the application components
and interaction among them,

00:49:41.851 --> 00:49:45.340
then you can decide how to
place them in the continuum,

00:49:45.341 --> 00:49:50.835
both horizontally as well as
vertically in this continuum.

00:49:50.836 --> 00:49:52.610
So that's part of what

00:49:52.611 --> 00:49:55.965
this foglets distributed
programming model does is,

00:49:55.966 --> 00:49:58.695
is respecting the
application properties,

00:49:58.696 --> 00:50:01.040
it places the application
components in

00:50:01.041 --> 00:50:03.735
the hierarchy in the continuum.

00:50:03.736 --> 00:50:05.510
But that's not a one-time thing

00:50:05.511 --> 00:50:07.570
because you can have
sensors moving,

00:50:07.571 --> 00:50:09.990
and if sensors move then

00:50:09.991 --> 00:50:12.120
the Fog node that used
to be processing may

00:50:12.121 --> 00:50:14.375
not be the appropriate one
to continue the processing.

00:50:14.376 --> 00:50:16.185
Which means that you have to

00:50:16.186 --> 00:50:18.425
migrate the application component

00:50:18.426 --> 00:50:20.580
to a node that is

00:50:20.581 --> 00:50:22.715
close to where
the production of data is.

00:50:22.716 --> 00:50:25.640
So, that transparent
state migration

00:50:25.641 --> 00:50:28.720
is part of foglets
programming model as well,

00:50:28.721 --> 00:50:32.090
and then multi-tenancy is

00:50:32.091 --> 00:50:34.715
something that I
already mentioned.

00:50:34.716 --> 00:50:38.120
So, once you migrate
something to a different Fog

00:50:38.121 --> 00:50:41.560
node but you've left
some breadcrumbs over here,

00:50:41.561 --> 00:50:43.020
and those breadcrumbs
are rebrought

00:50:43.021 --> 00:50:44.900
over in order to continue
the computation.

00:50:44.901 --> 00:50:46.740
That's part of
the programming model

00:50:46.741 --> 00:50:49.280
in terms of transparently
worrying about

00:50:49.281 --> 00:50:51.850
moving both the
computation as well as

00:50:51.851 --> 00:50:54.385
the state of those
distributed computation,

00:50:54.386 --> 00:50:57.370
that's what this foglets
programming model gives you,

00:50:57.371 --> 00:51:00.440
and if you're interested
in that please look at

00:51:00.441 --> 00:51:02.880
this publication and I'll

00:51:02.881 --> 00:51:04.525
be happy to talk to
you also about that.

00:51:04.526 --> 00:51:06.895
The second thing that
I want to mention is

00:51:06.896 --> 00:51:12.950
the second project relates to
the recovery aspect of it.

00:51:12.951 --> 00:51:16.030
So, when you have data
that is very important,

00:51:16.031 --> 00:51:18.340
then you want to make sure
that the data doesn't go

00:51:18.341 --> 00:51:21.810
away when there is
failures, right?

00:51:22.360 --> 00:51:26.965
So, the latency is
also a consideration.

00:51:26.966 --> 00:51:28.480
So, what we want to do is,

00:51:28.481 --> 00:51:30.160
we want to replicate
the data in order

00:51:30.161 --> 00:51:32.285
to worry about
recovery properties.

00:51:32.286 --> 00:51:33.850
So, that's okay, go ahead

00:51:33.851 --> 00:51:35.510
and replicate it in
a particular way so

00:51:35.511 --> 00:51:36.800
that I can make sure that

00:51:36.801 --> 00:51:39.580
the latency that is
important is preserved.

00:51:39.581 --> 00:51:42.100
But if I do this, what's going
to happen is if there is

00:51:42.101 --> 00:51:44.755
a correlated failure that

00:51:44.756 --> 00:51:47.390
all of the data is
lost, therefore,

00:51:47.391 --> 00:51:49.750
what I might want to do is
I might want to replicate

00:51:49.751 --> 00:51:53.180
the data far away from

00:51:53.181 --> 00:51:55.270
the current location of where

00:51:55.271 --> 00:51:57.300
the data is being
generated so that I can

00:51:57.301 --> 00:52:01.085
have a resiliency and
recovery properties,

00:52:01.086 --> 00:52:02.810
but that results in poor latency.

00:52:02.811 --> 00:52:05.045
So, there is this consistency

00:52:05.046 --> 00:52:08.375
and latency trade-off
that exists.

00:52:08.376 --> 00:52:10.290
When you think about some of

00:52:10.291 --> 00:52:12.595
these frameworks that are
available in the Cloud,

00:52:12.596 --> 00:52:14.660
what it'll let you do
is it will say, "Well,

00:52:14.661 --> 00:52:16.370
we're going to give you
eventual consistency,

00:52:16.371 --> 00:52:17.780
that's all we're
going to give you.

00:52:17.781 --> 00:52:20.185
If you care about
strong consistency,

00:52:20.186 --> 00:52:21.805
you look at it in
the application.

00:52:21.806 --> 00:52:24.220
If there was a violation
of strong consistency

00:52:24.221 --> 00:52:26.930
you have to take corrective
actions in the application."

00:52:26.931 --> 00:52:28.660
That to me is not
very satisfying.

00:52:28.661 --> 00:52:30.800
So, one of the things that
my students came up with

00:52:30.801 --> 00:52:33.430
this idea of a context
of interest.

00:52:33.431 --> 00:52:34.770
So, the context of interest

00:52:34.771 --> 00:52:36.350
says that this is a context in

00:52:36.351 --> 00:52:39.310
which I want very
strong guarantees in the data.

00:52:39.311 --> 00:52:41.385
Meaning, that I want to know that

00:52:41.386 --> 00:52:44.185
anything that I read is
most up-to-date value, right?

00:52:44.186 --> 00:52:47.260
So, that's a strong consistency

00:52:47.261 --> 00:52:49.965
is being provided in
the context of interest.

00:52:49.966 --> 00:52:51.295
But from the point of view

00:52:51.296 --> 00:52:54.795
avoiding correlated failures,
what we're going to do is,

00:52:54.796 --> 00:52:57.240
we're going to also replicate
it in a faraway node,

00:52:57.241 --> 00:52:59.355
but we don't give you
strong guarantees on that,

00:52:59.356 --> 00:53:01.550
so we give you eventual
consistency for that.

00:53:01.551 --> 00:53:07.300
So, that's the idea behind
this FogStore work, and again,

00:53:07.301 --> 00:53:09.050
only a bird's eye view of

00:53:09.051 --> 00:53:10.690
the details in
that paper but if you're

00:53:10.691 --> 00:53:14.055
interested in that you
can look at this paper.

00:53:14.056 --> 00:53:15.840
So, that's the second
thing that is looking

00:53:15.841 --> 00:53:18.235
at the latency properties.

00:53:18.236 --> 00:53:21.915
The third thing is
the fact that you have

00:53:21.916 --> 00:53:24.710
Fog resources and alluded to this

00:53:24.711 --> 00:53:27.585
that the resources are
not multiple racks.

00:53:27.586 --> 00:53:30.730
You have a limited amount of
resources and you are having

00:53:30.731 --> 00:53:35.020
applications that are continuously
generating data 24-7.

00:53:35.030 --> 00:53:37.870
In that situation,
what you have to worry

00:53:37.871 --> 00:53:40.115
about is how to
place this data that

00:53:40.116 --> 00:53:43.045
is being generated so that
you can make sure that

00:53:43.046 --> 00:53:44.720
it scales and it

00:53:44.721 --> 00:53:46.600
also provides you
low-latency properties,

00:53:46.601 --> 00:53:49.370
all of these good things
that you need to do.

00:53:49.371 --> 00:53:52.100
So, that's the idea
behind this DataFog,

00:53:52.101 --> 00:53:53.830
and in particular what

00:53:53.831 --> 00:53:56.355
happens is that there could
be skews in the workload.

00:53:56.356 --> 00:53:58.490
Going back to the game
example that you had,

00:53:58.491 --> 00:54:02.200
as soon as the game ends,

00:54:02.201 --> 00:54:03.740
everybody's leaving the stadium.

00:54:03.741 --> 00:54:05.810
So, it is going to
result in a huge amount

00:54:05.811 --> 00:54:08.395
of activity around that area,

00:54:08.396 --> 00:54:10.700
and in that case,
the Edge infrastructure

00:54:10.701 --> 00:54:12.380
here is going to
be oversubscribed.

00:54:12.381 --> 00:54:15.700
So, what we have in DataFog
is ways by which we can say,

00:54:15.701 --> 00:54:17.730
what is the data that we need

00:54:17.731 --> 00:54:20.045
for immediate query answering?

00:54:20.046 --> 00:54:22.670
So, these are real-time queries
can be answered by that,

00:54:22.671 --> 00:54:25.120
and the older data
can be moved up in

00:54:25.121 --> 00:54:29.030
the hierarchy to
ultimately to the Cloud,

00:54:29.031 --> 00:54:31.930
and so that's the way we think

00:54:31.931 --> 00:54:36.490
about capacity conscious
data replication.

00:54:36.491 --> 00:54:38.825
There are details of, again,

00:54:38.826 --> 00:54:40.260
on how we do that,

00:54:40.261 --> 00:54:42.810
taking both the
spatio-temporal coordinates

00:54:42.811 --> 00:54:44.640
of the location of

00:54:44.641 --> 00:54:46.690
the data and then
figuring out how

00:54:46.691 --> 00:54:49.340
to displays the data in
terms of replication.

00:54:49.341 --> 00:54:51.170
So, details of that you can see

00:54:51.171 --> 00:54:53.900
in a publication that recently
came on board as well.

00:54:53.901 --> 00:54:56.360
So, those are the examples I

00:54:56.361 --> 00:54:59.435
have of things that we're
doing in the infrastructure,

00:54:59.436 --> 00:55:02.190
but I want to give you
a couple of examples of

00:55:02.191 --> 00:55:06.605
applications that might
use autonomous Edge.

00:55:06.606 --> 00:55:08.890
So, for this, I'm
going to give you

00:55:08.891 --> 00:55:11.554
an example of creating

00:55:11.555 --> 00:55:16.460
Space Time Trajectory of
vehicles on the roadways.

00:55:16.461 --> 00:55:18.700
Now, the assumption
is that you know

00:55:18.701 --> 00:55:21.480
already the layout of
cameras in the roadways

00:55:21.481 --> 00:55:25.070
and so in principle might seem

00:55:25.071 --> 00:55:26.680
like if I wanted to record

00:55:26.681 --> 00:55:29.265
the activity of
all the vehicles all the time,

00:55:29.266 --> 00:55:30.735
it seems like a lot of data.

00:55:30.736 --> 00:55:33.800
But in practice,
what is happening

00:55:33.801 --> 00:55:36.785
is every car starts
its journey somewhere,

00:55:36.786 --> 00:55:38.025
ends it's somewhere, right?

00:55:38.026 --> 00:55:39.590
So, it's an activity and

00:55:39.591 --> 00:55:41.635
the number of
activities is bounded

00:55:41.636 --> 00:55:45.925
by traffic conditions and where
you want to go and so on,

00:55:45.926 --> 00:55:47.795
and so that's
the intuition behind

00:55:47.796 --> 00:55:50.120
how we can do this even

00:55:50.121 --> 00:55:55.565
though we want do this
continuously for all the vehicles.

00:55:55.566 --> 00:55:58.500
So, the idea is that

00:55:59.780 --> 00:56:03.085
the camera that are
attached to Edge nodes

00:56:03.086 --> 00:56:05.930
and in a particular roadway,

00:56:05.931 --> 00:56:07.320
I sight a particular vehicle,

00:56:07.321 --> 00:56:09.105
and I can say this
is the geocoordinate

00:56:09.106 --> 00:56:10.480
of this vehicle and this is

00:56:10.481 --> 00:56:14.240
the time at which I
saw this vehicle here.

00:56:14.241 --> 00:56:15.890
As soon as I know this,

00:56:15.891 --> 00:56:17.345
I can communicate to

00:56:17.346 --> 00:56:20.610
other cameras that are
downstream from me.

00:56:20.611 --> 00:56:23.240
Given the direction of
motion of the vehicle and

00:56:23.241 --> 00:56:26.385
the layout of the cameras
in the roadways,

00:56:26.386 --> 00:56:29.665
and I can tell
the downstream cameras that,

00:56:29.666 --> 00:56:30.800
"Look out for this vehicle."

00:56:30.801 --> 00:56:32.200
I'm sending a signature of

00:56:32.201 --> 00:56:34.180
the vehicle to look for and it is

00:56:34.181 --> 00:56:36.565
the responsibility of
the downstream nodes

00:56:36.566 --> 00:56:40.790
to re-identify when that
particular vehicle comes along.

00:56:40.791 --> 00:56:43.510
So, when that particular
vehicle comes along,

00:56:43.511 --> 00:56:45.125
it can record that, "Well,

00:56:45.126 --> 00:56:47.165
I found this vehicle at

00:56:47.166 --> 00:56:53.900
the geolocation G_2 and
a particular time T_2.

00:56:53.901 --> 00:56:55.280
Once I have that,

00:56:55.281 --> 00:56:58.355
once these guys can confirm
that yes, they found it,

00:56:58.356 --> 00:57:01.830
then I can actually send
this partial trajectory that I

00:57:01.831 --> 00:57:05.065
have to this guy and get
rid of it locally, right?

00:57:05.066 --> 00:57:06.760
I don't have to hold it anymore.

00:57:06.761 --> 00:57:09.370
So, you can aggregate all of

00:57:09.371 --> 00:57:14.290
these trajectories and
keep it together and,

00:57:14.291 --> 00:57:16.495
of course, you can say, "Well,

00:57:16.496 --> 00:57:19.150
we may not be able to keep
all the trajectories at

00:57:19.151 --> 00:57:20.330
one point and you can

00:57:20.331 --> 00:57:22.760
do either lazy
aggregation and so on."

00:57:22.761 --> 00:57:24.210
We'll talk about
that in a minute.

00:57:24.211 --> 00:57:27.120
Also, as you keep on getting

00:57:27.121 --> 00:57:30.065
all of these trajectories
accumulated here,

00:57:30.066 --> 00:57:31.180
you might say that
some of these are

00:57:31.181 --> 00:57:33.520
old trajectories and
recent queries for

00:57:33.521 --> 00:57:37.130
recent tracks in which case I can

00:57:37.131 --> 00:57:42.375
even push it into the Cloud
and not do that and we can do

00:57:42.376 --> 00:57:44.980
lazy trajectory aggregation so

00:57:44.981 --> 00:57:46.930
that you can keep the footprint

00:57:46.931 --> 00:57:53.180
of storage requirement in
each of the nodes reasonable.

00:57:53.200 --> 00:57:55.960
As I said, the storage
is bounded by

00:57:55.961 --> 00:57:58.560
the activities that is
at a particular camera,

00:57:58.561 --> 00:57:59.995
and that's the reason that it is

00:57:59.996 --> 00:58:02.175
something that is scalable
and it can do it.

00:58:02.176 --> 00:58:04.630
Again, this is
only a bird's eye view

00:58:04.631 --> 00:58:07.405
of what this paper does.

00:58:07.406 --> 00:58:09.665
If you're interested, talk to me.

00:58:09.666 --> 00:58:12.050
What we are doing right
now is we're working

00:58:12.051 --> 00:58:17.110
with campus security folks.

00:58:17.111 --> 00:58:20.010
I'm sure that this is something
that most of you have

00:58:20.011 --> 00:58:22.480
experienced that every
campus has cameras,

00:58:22.481 --> 00:58:24.640
and I asked my security guy,

00:58:24.641 --> 00:58:25.470
"What do you do with the camera?"

00:58:25.471 --> 00:58:27.680
"Oh, we have a Google map and
we can click on the camera

00:58:27.681 --> 00:58:30.325
and look at what is happening.
There is no intelligence."

00:58:30.326 --> 00:58:31.740
We asked them what are the use

00:58:31.741 --> 00:58:33.000
cases that they
are interested in,

00:58:33.001 --> 00:58:34.505
and we can actually track

00:58:34.506 --> 00:58:37.080
vehicles in real-time
and then record that.

00:58:37.081 --> 00:58:40.095
So that's what we're doing
currently with that.

00:58:40.096 --> 00:58:42.425
Another example that
I wanted to give

00:58:42.426 --> 00:58:45.685
is in the context
of social sensing,

00:58:45.686 --> 00:58:48.265
and what we have done is

00:58:48.266 --> 00:58:50.120
we have multiple components

00:58:50.121 --> 00:58:51.550
in the application, and again,

00:58:51.551 --> 00:58:56.595
deployed it across the continuum

00:58:56.596 --> 00:58:58.375
and you can have
network partitions,

00:58:58.376 --> 00:58:59.915
and if you have
network partitions,

00:58:59.916 --> 00:59:05.305
then you can reconfigure and
resume the operation and

00:59:05.306 --> 00:59:08.800
opportunistically we can
communicate when it is possible

00:59:08.801 --> 00:59:12.560
using drones or buses that
carry Edge nodes and so on;

00:59:12.561 --> 00:59:13.875
we can communicate with that.

00:59:13.876 --> 00:59:17.200
This is, again, something that we

00:59:17.201 --> 00:59:18.980
have done before and if you're

00:59:18.981 --> 00:59:21.055
interested you can look
at this publication.

00:59:21.056 --> 00:59:23.980
So, there is a bunch

00:59:23.981 --> 00:59:25.760
of ongoing work and I
won't talk about that,

00:59:25.761 --> 00:59:28.510
but if you're interested
I can do that because

00:59:28.511 --> 00:59:32.100
I've been asked to wrap
up and so I'll wrap up.

00:59:32.101 --> 00:59:35.530
So, in the final comment
that I want to make is

00:59:35.531 --> 00:59:38.660
that I hope whether it
is propagated or not,

00:59:38.661 --> 00:59:40.840
but I hope I have communicated

00:59:40.841 --> 00:59:44.870
that we want to make the
Edge as a peer of the Cloud.

00:59:45.060 --> 00:59:50.270
Victor articulated and how
this is a disruption that is

00:59:50.271 --> 00:59:53.510
coming and in order

00:59:53.511 --> 00:59:55.620
to position ourselves
for the disruption,

00:59:55.621 --> 00:59:57.480
this is the thing that we

00:59:57.481 --> 01:00:00.330
want to be looking
at, okay? Thank you.

01:00:04.320 --> 01:00:07.050
&gt;&gt; Same thing anybody have

01:00:07.051 --> 01:00:14.150
any initial thoughts on the doc?

01:00:14.151 --> 01:00:17.200
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] kind of vision.
The question I would ask,

01:00:17.201 --> 01:00:20.029
Satya talks about
Microsoft as a company

01:00:20.030 --> 01:00:22.935
that can be trusted with
privacy guarantees.

01:00:22.936 --> 01:00:28.030
I was.

01:00:28.031 --> 01:00:28.595
&gt;&gt; I can hear.

01:00:28.596 --> 01:00:31.310
&gt;&gt; Privacy guarantees
security for data.

01:00:31.311 --> 01:00:35.635
I was sort of expecting between
the two talks so far that

01:00:35.636 --> 01:00:38.250
somebody would start
to talk a little bit

01:00:38.251 --> 01:00:41.030
about the privacy
implications of Edge,

01:00:41.031 --> 01:00:42.475
and of course here you are

01:00:42.476 --> 01:00:44.795
looking at who might
be driving a car,

01:00:44.796 --> 01:00:46.890
where they're driving
to and who's with them.

01:00:46.891 --> 01:00:48.905
That's very clearly
in this space.

01:00:48.906 --> 01:00:50.470
So, I'm just curious to know what

01:00:50.471 --> 01:00:52.980
your thoughts are
about a part of this.

01:00:52.981 --> 01:00:56.280
&gt;&gt; That's a very valid concern
so for instance,

01:00:56.281 --> 01:00:57.610
when I talked to the campus

01:00:57.611 --> 01:00:59.030
security folks they said, well,

01:00:59.031 --> 01:01:03.160
are you going to publish these
camera feeds on YouTube?

01:01:03.161 --> 01:01:04.810
I said No. I'm just using it for

01:01:04.811 --> 01:01:07.755
analysis and then recording it,

01:01:07.756 --> 01:01:09.440
and we're not doing
anything more with that.

01:01:09.441 --> 01:01:10.980
But absolutely right that

01:01:10.981 --> 01:01:13.540
privacy considerations
is super important,

01:01:13.541 --> 01:01:15.020
and we have
a very strong group at

01:01:15.021 --> 01:01:16.920
Georgia Tech that is
looking at security and

01:01:16.921 --> 01:01:18.960
privacy issues and I hope
to partner with them

01:01:18.961 --> 01:01:21.235
in terms of looking at
some of those issues.

01:01:21.236 --> 01:01:23.325
&gt;&gt; Okay. On that note
thank you very much.

01:01:23.326 --> 01:01:27.070
Ken, just one comment I'll
make to your comment is that,

01:01:27.071 --> 01:01:29.785
we've been dealing with
traffic cameras quite a bit

01:01:29.786 --> 01:01:32.990
and every city deals
with it differently.

01:01:32.991 --> 01:01:34.630
Some of them for example Seattle,

01:01:34.631 --> 01:01:36.290
just puts it on
the web, you can go and

01:01:36.291 --> 01:01:38.290
look at it. Anybody can.

01:01:38.291 --> 01:01:41.060
Other places like Belleview
will not put it on the web

01:01:41.061 --> 01:01:42.170
and then we have to sort of sign

01:01:42.171 --> 01:01:43.745
some sort of agreement with them.

01:01:43.746 --> 01:01:46.110
So there is no clarity yet on

01:01:46.111 --> 01:01:49.085
how people are dealing with
it because it's policy.

01:01:49.086 --> 01:01:51.600
Anyway, beyond that real pleasure

