01:02:30.646 --> 01:02:35.090
he has a PhD from Purdue
so among his alumni.

01:02:37.650 --> 01:02:41.680
I wanted to ask him
to share with you,

01:02:41.681 --> 01:02:43.845
our existing, current vision

01:02:43.846 --> 01:02:46.515
of where we are headed with
all this stuff, so Arjmand.

01:02:46.516 --> 01:02:48.205
&gt;&gt; Thank you, Victor.

01:02:48.206 --> 01:02:49.700
It's a pleasure to be here.

01:02:49.701 --> 01:02:51.625
Thank you, Victor,
for the invitation.

01:02:51.626 --> 01:02:53.725
I've been working
with a bunch of,

01:02:53.726 --> 01:02:55.295
I see very familiar faces,

01:02:55.296 --> 01:02:56.590
quite a few, and so I've been

01:02:56.591 --> 01:02:59.315
working with the community
for some time.

01:02:59.316 --> 01:03:02.280
I moved off to Azure IoT which is

01:03:02.281 --> 01:03:05.050
the Azure engineering team
responsible for

01:03:05.051 --> 01:03:08.880
IoT about 3 and a half
years back.

01:03:08.881 --> 01:03:15.030
I lead the Azure IoT
Edge engineering.

01:03:15.031 --> 01:03:20.090
So my team is responsible
for making Edge real and

01:03:20.091 --> 01:03:22.270
the one message I bring to

01:03:22.271 --> 01:03:25.510
this group here is that
Edge is really real.

01:03:25.511 --> 01:03:27.640
There are actual customers and

01:03:27.641 --> 01:03:29.580
I'm going to actually
talk about some of those,

01:03:29.581 --> 01:03:31.955
there are actual
customers who use Edge,

01:03:31.956 --> 01:03:33.690
who see the value in the Edge and

01:03:33.691 --> 01:03:36.395
the Cloud and they
marry the two together.

01:03:36.396 --> 01:03:38.660
So there's a lot of
momentum that we see out

01:03:38.661 --> 01:03:40.820
there and I'm going
to talk about that.

01:03:40.821 --> 01:03:44.300
So, when Victor asked me I
should come here I said "what

01:03:44.301 --> 01:03:47.995
better thing to do than to
bring in some real scenarios,

01:03:47.996 --> 01:03:50.960
some real actual customer
use cases and

01:03:50.961 --> 01:03:52.560
talk about some of the challenges

01:03:52.561 --> 01:03:54.795
that they see are customers".

01:03:54.796 --> 01:03:57.950
Then really ask you,

01:03:57.951 --> 01:04:00.710
the community to solve
some of those challenges.

01:04:00.711 --> 01:04:02.890
So let's make it real.

01:04:02.891 --> 01:04:06.050
I'm going to give
you a little bit

01:04:06.051 --> 01:04:10.010
of an introduction into
the IoT patterns that we see.

01:04:10.011 --> 01:04:13.900
This is how we right now
see of course the Cloud

01:04:13.901 --> 01:04:17.380
is there and when we make
a slide with a Cloud,

01:04:17.381 --> 01:04:19.850
the Cloud takes 75 percent

01:04:19.851 --> 01:04:21.690
of the space, that's by design.

01:04:21.691 --> 01:04:24.355
But as you would also come here.

01:04:24.356 --> 01:04:27.050
We look at IoT as things.

01:04:27.051 --> 01:04:28.700
These are things
around us there are

01:04:28.701 --> 01:04:30.285
things here there are things,

01:04:30.286 --> 01:04:31.785
the vehicle is a thing,

01:04:31.786 --> 01:04:34.590
smart cities,
manufacturing machines,

01:04:34.591 --> 01:04:36.770
robotic arms, they're all things.

01:04:36.771 --> 01:04:39.060
Things do two things
they generate

01:04:39.061 --> 01:04:41.900
data and they can be actuated,

01:04:41.901 --> 01:04:44.500
they can be controlled.
So, these are things.

01:04:44.501 --> 01:04:49.085
Another thing we look at it
in three ways is insights.

01:04:49.086 --> 01:04:51.630
When these things generate
data there's a lot

01:04:51.631 --> 01:04:54.430
of insights that you
can get from the data.

01:04:54.431 --> 01:04:56.940
You can understand what is
the state of the machine,

01:04:56.941 --> 01:04:59.110
you can understand the state of

01:04:59.111 --> 01:05:01.500
the environment in which
the machine is operating,

01:05:01.501 --> 01:05:04.320
you can also because
now you have insights,

01:05:04.321 --> 01:05:06.395
you can have some actions.

01:05:06.396 --> 01:05:08.850
You can do some actions
on the machine on

01:05:08.851 --> 01:05:11.970
the thing or you can take
actions on your end,

01:05:11.971 --> 01:05:14.040
you can adapt
some processes and so.

01:05:14.041 --> 01:05:15.755
So that's how we look at this,

01:05:15.756 --> 01:05:20.240
data of course flows as we
see it today from the things

01:05:20.241 --> 01:05:22.910
to insights in the Cloud and from

01:05:22.911 --> 01:05:24.100
there to actions and then

01:05:24.101 --> 01:05:26.570
maybe actions come
back to the Cloud.

01:05:26.571 --> 01:05:31.655
But then now if you put
an Edge concept on top of it,

01:05:31.656 --> 01:05:34.785
you have- I'll skip this.

01:05:34.786 --> 01:05:36.760
You have the same thing,

01:05:36.761 --> 01:05:38.020
you have the same insights,

01:05:38.021 --> 01:05:39.400
you have the actions
and you of course have

01:05:39.401 --> 01:05:41.965
a Cloud gateway but
what you now do

01:05:41.966 --> 01:05:46.835
here is you move some of
the insights you get from data,

01:05:46.836 --> 01:05:49.060
some of the actions
that you need to do

01:05:49.061 --> 01:05:52.000
and you bring it right
there on the thing.

01:05:52.001 --> 01:05:54.060
Now your thing the robotic arm,

01:05:54.061 --> 01:05:56.575
the huge milling machine,

01:05:56.576 --> 01:05:58.470
your car, your smart city,

01:05:58.471 --> 01:06:00.730
your environment like
this conference room,

01:06:00.731 --> 01:06:03.870
they have become smart
in themselves because

01:06:03.871 --> 01:06:07.015
those insights which essentially
is AI machine learning,

01:06:07.016 --> 01:06:10.160
parts of those have been
moved and to the thing.

01:06:10.161 --> 01:06:12.655
That's how we look
at the Edge because

01:06:12.656 --> 01:06:15.475
Edge and the Cloud work together.

01:06:15.476 --> 01:06:18.300
Now I won't go over
some of the virtues of

01:06:18.301 --> 01:06:21.125
the Cloud and some of
the virtues of the Edge,

01:06:21.126 --> 01:06:23.400
only to say that both of

01:06:23.401 --> 01:06:25.965
them are important
in their own self.

01:06:25.966 --> 01:06:29.500
You cannot do a huge machine

01:06:29.501 --> 01:06:32.070
learning training exercise
with tons of data from

01:06:32.071 --> 01:06:34.610
distributed nodes coming
and you only need

01:06:34.611 --> 01:06:38.090
the Cloud to do that because
of near-infinite resources.

01:06:38.091 --> 01:06:40.875
But then at the same time
if you're looking for

01:06:40.876 --> 01:06:43.910
near real time latency,

01:06:43.911 --> 01:06:45.790
you are looking for
control loops which

01:06:45.791 --> 01:06:47.820
can run reliably right next to

01:06:47.821 --> 01:06:50.355
the machine you are not
going to send calls

01:06:50.356 --> 01:06:53.245
up to the Cloud and wait
for them to come back,

01:06:53.246 --> 01:06:54.760
which might not be reliable.

01:06:54.761 --> 01:06:56.630
You need those insights,

01:06:56.631 --> 01:06:59.725
those AI machine learning
right on that machine.

01:06:59.726 --> 01:07:03.620
Now, we can talk a lot
about these these virtues

01:07:03.621 --> 01:07:05.370
of the Cloud and the
Edge and how they

01:07:05.371 --> 01:07:07.635
sit together and how
they augment each other,

01:07:07.636 --> 01:07:09.485
but the way we have done

01:07:09.486 --> 01:07:11.640
now coming to what we
have done the product

01:07:11.641 --> 01:07:14.210
is to create a consistency

01:07:14.211 --> 01:07:16.410
between the Cloud and the Edge.

01:07:16.411 --> 01:07:18.010
By consistency we mean

01:07:18.011 --> 01:07:20.165
the consistency in
programming models,

01:07:20.166 --> 01:07:22.725
consistency in the
application model.

01:07:22.726 --> 01:07:24.090
What does that mean?

01:07:24.091 --> 01:07:25.340
That means that if there is

01:07:25.341 --> 01:07:28.285
a workload that you're
running in the Cloud.

01:07:28.286 --> 01:07:30.865
Imagine a Stream Analytics job

01:07:30.866 --> 01:07:33.140
which is ingesting tons of data,

01:07:33.141 --> 01:07:34.650
analyzing the data and then

01:07:34.651 --> 01:07:36.320
coming out with some statistics,

01:07:36.321 --> 01:07:40.615
the same application model we
have created for the Edge.

01:07:40.616 --> 01:07:45.274
Which has implications
beyond just the model,

01:07:45.275 --> 01:07:46.870
which means you can actually move

01:07:46.871 --> 01:07:48.870
that same workload without

01:07:48.871 --> 01:07:51.300
any change from the Cloud

01:07:51.301 --> 01:07:54.199
onto the Edge without
any modifications,

01:07:54.200 --> 01:07:57.550
without writing any code
all through configurations.

01:07:57.551 --> 01:07:59.720
So that is the consistency,

01:07:59.721 --> 01:08:01.400
that is how consistency between

01:08:01.401 --> 01:08:03.170
the Cloud programming model and

01:08:03.171 --> 01:08:04.610
the application model and

01:08:04.611 --> 01:08:06.410
the Edge programming model

01:08:06.411 --> 01:08:08.490
and application model helps us.

01:08:08.491 --> 01:08:11.290
It has implications
beyond just technology,

01:08:11.291 --> 01:08:13.770
it has organizational
implications.

01:08:13.771 --> 01:08:17.110
So now if you do not
have this consistency in

01:08:17.111 --> 01:08:20.060
the programming model,
you need developers,

01:08:20.061 --> 01:08:21.800
you need people who
actually program

01:08:21.801 --> 01:08:23.900
the Cloud application and then

01:08:23.901 --> 01:08:25.910
you need people who
actually go in and

01:08:25.911 --> 01:08:28.225
program the Edge application.

01:08:28.226 --> 01:08:31.480
But the consistency and an
infrastructure which allows

01:08:31.481 --> 01:08:34.710
you to move workloads from
the Cloud to the Edge,

01:08:34.711 --> 01:08:36.790
you do not need
that you just need

01:08:36.791 --> 01:08:38.620
Cloud developers who develop

01:08:38.621 --> 01:08:40.810
their applications in the Cloud,

01:08:40.811 --> 01:08:42.840
make parts of it which they want

01:08:42.841 --> 01:08:44.720
or they think or
they optimized to

01:08:44.721 --> 01:08:46.260
run on the Edge and simply

01:08:46.261 --> 01:08:48.815
configure it that way and
then they can move it back.

01:08:48.816 --> 01:08:51.675
So that's really how
we look at the Edge

01:08:51.676 --> 01:08:54.695
that is what Azure IoT Edge does.

01:08:54.696 --> 01:08:57.185
By the way this is
Azure IoT Edge,

01:08:57.186 --> 01:08:59.110
there's also another product

01:08:59.111 --> 01:09:01.150
the Edge browser this is not it,

01:09:01.151 --> 01:09:05.220
this is Azure IoT Edge
which we're talking about.

01:09:05.221 --> 01:09:06.420
Okay so now I'm going to make it

01:09:06.421 --> 01:09:07.940
a little bit real
then I'm going to

01:09:07.941 --> 01:09:09.690
stop a little bit and

01:09:09.691 --> 01:09:11.995
go into some design
principles here.

01:09:11.996 --> 01:09:14.690
This is a real customer
that we worked with.

01:09:14.691 --> 01:09:16.185
They have a mining operation.

01:09:16.186 --> 01:09:19.080
They have spread these
pumps which are spread

01:09:19.081 --> 01:09:22.490
around this very
remote locations.

01:09:22.491 --> 01:09:25.165
Many of these remote locations
are in the ocean,

01:09:25.166 --> 01:09:26.875
they're not even on land.

01:09:26.876 --> 01:09:28.800
Then they have one central place

01:09:28.801 --> 01:09:30.410
where they're controlling them.

01:09:30.411 --> 01:09:32.855
Network connectivity to
these remote locations

01:09:32.856 --> 01:09:35.200
is very bad or very expensive.

01:09:35.201 --> 01:09:37.720
So they typically would use
satellite connectivity.

01:09:37.721 --> 01:09:39.110
They can only get alerts

01:09:39.111 --> 01:09:41.700
otherwise it becomes
very expensive.

01:09:42.510 --> 01:09:48.500
Also reliability of
operation of this pump is

01:09:48.501 --> 01:09:50.840
extremely important
because if it goes

01:09:50.841 --> 01:09:54.425
down you lose money you
lose oil which is money.

01:09:54.426 --> 01:09:58.440
So this is their world,

01:09:58.441 --> 01:10:00.500
there is a pump
here which is going

01:10:00.501 --> 01:10:02.635
up and down similar to that one.

01:10:02.636 --> 01:10:04.760
The pump has really it's

01:10:04.761 --> 01:10:07.350
boiled down to
two values of pressure.

01:10:07.351 --> 01:10:10.115
Pressure above the plunger

01:10:10.116 --> 01:10:12.060
and pressure below the plunger.

01:10:12.061 --> 01:10:15.610
Then this on the X axis is
the position of the pump.

01:10:15.611 --> 01:10:18.060
So at all times there is

01:10:18.061 --> 01:10:21.000
an envelope of operation
for these two pressures,

01:10:21.001 --> 01:10:23.820
the minute it goes
out of scope out of

01:10:23.821 --> 01:10:25.320
that envelope this pump

01:10:25.321 --> 01:10:28.695
fails and this whole
operation goes down.

01:10:28.696 --> 01:10:30.130
Now obviously there is

01:10:30.131 --> 01:10:32.400
a requirement for it
to be near real time,

01:10:32.401 --> 01:10:33.810
so they need to monitor this,

01:10:33.811 --> 01:10:36.510
there's also a requirement
to be predictive.

01:10:36.511 --> 01:10:38.740
You cannot wait for
this envelope to be

01:10:38.741 --> 01:10:41.095
broken to take some action.

01:10:41.096 --> 01:10:44.260
You need to predict when
this would happen so that

01:10:44.261 --> 01:10:45.760
it does not break down and

01:10:45.761 --> 01:10:48.140
you can do something
about this pump.

01:10:48.141 --> 01:10:50.175
You want to do it in offline.

01:10:50.176 --> 01:10:52.250
This pump could be
operating in the middle of

01:10:52.251 --> 01:10:53.450
the ocean and you do not

01:10:53.451 --> 01:10:55.585
have any connectivity
to the Cloud.

01:10:55.586 --> 01:10:58.370
Now how they do it is right now,

01:10:58.371 --> 01:10:59.600
they would have the pump.

01:10:59.601 --> 01:11:02.680
They actually have
some automation they call it,

01:11:02.681 --> 01:11:04.820
there's a gateway, there's
a SCADA controller,

01:11:04.821 --> 01:11:07.500
there's an HMI right next to it.

01:11:07.970 --> 01:11:10.000
Then there's an alert there's

01:11:10.001 --> 01:11:12.245
a supervisor sitting
in the central place,

01:11:12.246 --> 01:11:14.130
there is an alert
which is generated

01:11:14.131 --> 01:11:16.310
when that envelope is broken

01:11:16.311 --> 01:11:18.305
and then the supervisor would

01:11:18.306 --> 01:11:20.560
send somebody who would
start tweaking the pump,

01:11:20.561 --> 01:11:22.420
shutting it down or doing things

01:11:22.421 --> 01:11:24.840
which they can do to
mitigate that issue.

01:11:24.841 --> 01:11:28.090
So what they have now done and
of course moving that data

01:11:28.091 --> 01:11:29.820
up to the Cloud and
training machine learning

01:11:29.821 --> 01:11:31.030
and all is expensive,

01:11:31.031 --> 01:11:33.290
remember this satellite
connectivity.

01:11:33.291 --> 01:11:35.405
So, what they've done here,

01:11:35.406 --> 01:11:37.845
right next to
the existing gateway

01:11:37.846 --> 01:11:39.015
and the pump controller,

01:11:39.016 --> 01:11:41.430
they have deployed
Azure IoT Edge.

01:11:41.431 --> 01:11:44.475
This is right now on pumps
on mining operations.

01:11:44.476 --> 01:11:47.630
What it allows you to
do is to, first of all,

01:11:47.631 --> 01:11:49.340
have some translation between

01:11:49.341 --> 01:11:51.870
the protocols that are
happening that are used.

01:11:51.871 --> 01:11:54.550
Modbus is the protocol they
use and protocols which

01:11:54.551 --> 01:11:57.635
are more IoT-enabled like MQTT.

01:11:57.636 --> 01:11:59.790
But the second thing,
most important thing,

01:11:59.791 --> 01:12:02.155
you can actually deploy
machine learning

01:12:02.156 --> 01:12:04.950
right on the platform
on the pump,

01:12:04.951 --> 01:12:06.969
and now you could
be disconnected,

01:12:06.970 --> 01:12:09.445
and when that bad thing happens,

01:12:09.446 --> 01:12:11.275
you actually can control.

01:12:11.276 --> 01:12:12.545
Sorry, you can actually

01:12:12.546 --> 01:12:14.480
control this pump
right from there,

01:12:14.481 --> 01:12:16.790
you do not need to be
connected to the Cloud.

01:12:16.791 --> 01:12:18.360
Now this is a real scenario,

01:12:18.361 --> 01:12:20.360
this is what customers use today,

01:12:20.361 --> 01:12:22.325
Azure IoT Edge today.

01:12:22.326 --> 01:12:24.695
Another one, I just
have a few minutes.

01:12:24.696 --> 01:12:26.730
Another one is using

01:12:26.731 --> 01:12:30.950
drones for a number of
different operations,

01:12:30.951 --> 01:12:33.670
mostly vision video from

01:12:33.671 --> 01:12:38.165
drones and there are
a bunch of scenarios.

01:12:38.166 --> 01:12:40.020
Infrastructure
inspection is one of

01:12:40.021 --> 01:12:43.765
the most popular scenario
that we are seeing today.

01:12:43.766 --> 01:12:45.370
There are miles upon miles,

01:12:45.371 --> 01:12:48.420
hundreds of miles of
pipeline which is

01:12:48.421 --> 01:12:49.970
laid out there and it's

01:12:49.971 --> 01:12:52.020
physically not
possible for somebody,

01:12:52.021 --> 01:12:53.275
a technician, to go out and

01:12:53.276 --> 01:12:55.315
inspect every inch
of the pipeline.

01:12:55.316 --> 01:12:56.585
So what they do is,

01:12:56.586 --> 01:12:57.785
they've started to do is,

01:12:57.786 --> 01:13:00.140
fly drones over those pipelines

01:13:00.141 --> 01:13:03.020
and then take video
from the pipeline.

01:13:03.021 --> 01:13:04.450
Previously, what they
would do is they

01:13:04.451 --> 01:13:06.030
would collect a lot of video,

01:13:06.031 --> 01:13:07.180
bring it back to

01:13:07.181 --> 01:13:09.810
the base station and
then start analyzing.

01:13:09.811 --> 01:13:12.810
That, of course, meant
they had a latency of

01:13:12.811 --> 01:13:14.355
a few hours before
they would know

01:13:14.356 --> 01:13:15.980
that bad things are
happening there.

01:13:15.981 --> 01:13:17.305
So they're using,
and I'm going to go

01:13:17.306 --> 01:13:19.210
into the scenario
a little bit more.

01:13:19.211 --> 01:13:20.915
Smart agriculture;

01:13:20.916 --> 01:13:22.965
there's a lot of work
that has happened

01:13:22.966 --> 01:13:24.624
just to understand how

01:13:24.625 --> 01:13:26.590
the agriculture operation
is happening,

01:13:26.591 --> 01:13:27.810
drones are used there.

01:13:27.811 --> 01:13:30.600
Of course, our favorite
scenario which

01:13:30.601 --> 01:13:34.050
we like to debate a lot
about is surveillance.

01:13:34.051 --> 01:13:36.155
There's a lot of scenarios where,

01:13:36.156 --> 01:13:39.150
legitimately, you could
use drones there.

01:13:39.151 --> 01:13:44.085
So, Azure IoT Edge can actually
sit on top of a drone.

01:13:44.086 --> 01:13:45.970
The drone that it sits

01:13:45.971 --> 01:13:48.005
on- We have a
partnership with DJI,

01:13:48.006 --> 01:13:49.465
which is a major,

01:13:49.466 --> 01:13:52.040
one of the world's biggest
drone manufacturer.

01:13:52.041 --> 01:13:53.045
We worked with them.

01:13:53.046 --> 01:13:55.280
They put Azure IoT Edge
right on top of

01:13:55.281 --> 01:13:58.345
the drone and then through
connectivity with the Cloud,

01:13:58.346 --> 01:14:02.325
you can move your machine
learning onto that drone.

01:14:02.326 --> 01:14:04.285
Then these cheaper drones,

01:14:04.286 --> 01:14:07.160
this is about $1,000,
that's about $15,000.

01:14:07.161 --> 01:14:08.635
These cheaper drones, you

01:14:08.636 --> 01:14:10.295
actually can set
up a base station.

01:14:10.296 --> 01:14:11.975
They do not have
sufficient compute,

01:14:11.976 --> 01:14:15.285
more importantly, battery
to support Edge compute.

01:14:15.286 --> 01:14:19.160
So, you can actually deploy
it on a base station and then

01:14:19.161 --> 01:14:23.420
within that environment you
can do AI and ML and so on.

01:14:23.421 --> 01:14:27.930
So, these two are actually
soon they'll have an SDK,

01:14:27.931 --> 01:14:31.010
DJI will where you can actually
developers can develop

01:14:31.011 --> 01:14:35.335
using Azure IoT Edge to deploy
things onto the drones.

01:14:35.336 --> 01:14:37.795
How does the whole thing work?

01:14:37.796 --> 01:14:40.405
You have a video camera, which
is a drone video camera.

01:14:40.406 --> 01:14:43.210
We have IoT Edge
device on the drone.

01:14:43.211 --> 01:14:47.080
This is a little Nvidia chip
that sits there.

01:14:47.081 --> 01:14:48.530
On top of it, of course,

01:14:48.531 --> 01:14:50.085
in the Cloud, we have IoT Hub,

01:14:50.086 --> 01:14:51.200
and you have a bunch of

01:14:51.201 --> 01:14:54.615
your smarts AI and
ML in the Cloud.

01:14:54.616 --> 01:14:57.220
What you do is you
containerize them,

01:14:57.221 --> 01:14:59.854
we use Docker
container technology,

01:14:59.855 --> 01:15:03.885
you host it in the Cloud
in a container repo,

01:15:03.886 --> 01:15:07.900
you could use Azure Container
Registry or Docker repo.

01:15:07.901 --> 01:15:11.295
Then you could move
containers there,

01:15:11.296 --> 01:15:13.025
you build a manifest,

01:15:13.026 --> 01:15:14.475
you move it onto the drone,

01:15:14.476 --> 01:15:16.140
the manifest knows what are

01:15:16.141 --> 01:15:17.190
the different components you

01:15:17.191 --> 01:15:18.950
need to move down onto the drone.

01:15:18.951 --> 01:15:22.265
Then Azure IoT Edge runtime

01:15:22.266 --> 01:15:26.160
starts moving those containers
onto that platform.

01:15:26.161 --> 01:15:29.055
Then you could be
disconnected as long

01:15:29.056 --> 01:15:30.835
as you have the smarts

01:15:30.836 --> 01:15:32.795
which are required
for the drone to fly.

01:15:32.796 --> 01:15:34.490
You could be connected again,

01:15:34.491 --> 01:15:35.725
you could be doing it at scale.

01:15:35.726 --> 01:15:37.860
So, the whole value prop here is

01:15:37.861 --> 01:15:40.400
to actually allow you to
not deploy one drone,

01:15:40.401 --> 01:15:42.715
but to deploy thousands
upon thousands of

01:15:42.716 --> 01:15:45.140
drones and keep it updatable.

01:15:45.141 --> 01:15:46.995
You have a new machine
learning model,

01:15:46.996 --> 01:15:49.490
which you have retrained
based on data that you got,

01:15:49.491 --> 01:15:50.795
now you want to update

01:15:50.796 --> 01:15:52.730
your drone with a new
machine learning model.

01:15:52.731 --> 01:15:56.300
All you do is do a second
version of this container,

01:15:56.301 --> 01:15:57.480
push it up there,

01:15:57.481 --> 01:16:00.775
update the manifest, and now
you get the new version.

01:16:00.776 --> 01:16:06.560
So here are some design
principles that we follow.

01:16:06.561 --> 01:16:09.230
Of course, security is one
of the most important ones

01:16:09.231 --> 01:16:12.065
that are required for

01:16:12.066 --> 01:16:15.040
Edge Computing given
the devices out there.

01:16:15.041 --> 01:16:16.380
We have invested quite a lot,

01:16:16.381 --> 01:16:18.550
we have created
some partnerships,

01:16:18.551 --> 01:16:21.510
quite a few partnership
with Silicon Vendors,

01:16:21.511 --> 01:16:23.825
we have already announced,
and NXP microchip.

01:16:23.826 --> 01:16:26.300
They actually create
the right security frameworks

01:16:26.301 --> 01:16:28.090
for Edge to work.

01:16:28.091 --> 01:16:30.145
All of this is Cloud-managed

01:16:30.146 --> 01:16:31.350
while it can be disconnected.

01:16:31.351 --> 01:16:33.040
But to bootstrap their device,

01:16:33.041 --> 01:16:36.305
you need Cloud management
but it is done at scale.

01:16:36.306 --> 01:16:39.135
So, we can manage millions of

01:16:39.136 --> 01:16:40.505
IoT Edge devices which are

01:16:40.506 --> 01:16:42.425
deployed out there
from the Cloud.

01:16:42.426 --> 01:16:44.789
We can provision them
with zero-touch,

01:16:44.790 --> 01:16:47.055
we can also move configurations

01:16:47.056 --> 01:16:50.365
down onto those devices
with zero-touch.

01:16:50.366 --> 01:16:53.020
Cross-platform IoT Edge devices,

01:16:53.021 --> 01:16:54.670
you know the ones
we sat on top of

01:16:54.671 --> 01:16:57.675
the drones are
Windows, can be Linux,

01:16:57.676 --> 01:16:59.075
multiple versions of Linux,

01:16:59.076 --> 01:17:01.960
as long as the OS
supports containers,

01:17:01.961 --> 01:17:04.825
it can actually deploy
Azure IoT Edge.

01:17:04.826 --> 01:17:08.325
Portable, we have
a full Dev experience,

01:17:08.326 --> 01:17:10.605
CICD pipeline and so on for this.

01:17:10.606 --> 01:17:12.425
Then extensible, it's not

01:17:12.426 --> 01:17:15.455
only Azure services
that you deploy,

01:17:15.456 --> 01:17:17.220
not only Azure machine learning,

01:17:17.221 --> 01:17:19.775
not only Azure AI and so on.

01:17:19.776 --> 01:17:20.990
It could be your own code,

01:17:20.991 --> 01:17:23.910
it could be your own smart AI

01:17:23.911 --> 01:17:25.845
that you want to
deploy onto the Edge.

01:17:25.846 --> 01:17:28.110
You simply package
it as a container,

01:17:28.111 --> 01:17:31.240
put it somewhere, and
then it can be deployed.

01:17:31.241 --> 01:17:33.940
Can I finish and then I'll
come to the question?

01:17:33.941 --> 01:17:35.295
In fact, what we have done here,

01:17:35.296 --> 01:17:39.840
we have created a marketplace
for all of these modules,

01:17:39.841 --> 01:17:42.130
we call them modules, all
of these containers to be

01:17:42.131 --> 01:17:45.190
discoverable from where you
can deploy onto the Edge.

01:17:45.191 --> 01:17:47.525
I'll briefly touch on that.

01:17:47.526 --> 01:17:49.659
So, in terms of hardware,

01:17:49.660 --> 01:17:52.410
anywhere from
this interactive tier

01:17:52.411 --> 01:17:54.595
is something similar
to a Raspberry Pi,

01:17:54.596 --> 01:17:56.460
which can host Linux
in containers,

01:17:56.461 --> 01:17:58.185
all the way to
accelerate the tiers.

01:17:58.186 --> 01:18:02.205
These are FPGA-enabled,
GPU-accelerated hardware.

01:18:02.206 --> 01:18:04.470
IoT Edge can exist there.

01:18:04.471 --> 01:18:08.115
Here are open source. Azure
IoT Edge is on GitHub.

01:18:08.116 --> 01:18:09.880
MIT License totally open source,

01:18:09.881 --> 01:18:11.150
you can pick it up
and do whatever

01:18:11.151 --> 01:18:12.560
you want to do with it.

01:18:12.561 --> 01:18:16.320
It's Moby-based, so Docker
is based on Moby technology,

01:18:16.321 --> 01:18:17.685
and that's what it is based on.

01:18:17.686 --> 01:18:19.880
We have a marketplace
where you can actually

01:18:19.881 --> 01:18:22.505
go in and buy these
or get these modules.

01:18:22.506 --> 01:18:24.715
Security intelligence, we have

01:18:24.716 --> 01:18:26.750
a bunch of tooling,
different languages.

01:18:26.751 --> 01:18:28.110
We have five languages in which

01:18:28.111 --> 01:18:29.805
you can actually
write these modules;

01:18:29.806 --> 01:18:32.890
C, C#, Java, Node, Python.

01:18:32.891 --> 01:18:37.515
You can actually deploy it
in any of these languages.

01:18:37.516 --> 01:18:40.365
Since I have a couple
of more minutes,

01:18:40.366 --> 01:18:43.570
scale is one of the big
challenges that we face

01:18:43.571 --> 01:18:46.985
today, scale of deployment.

01:18:46.986 --> 01:18:50.395
These mining operations do
not work in onesies twosies,

01:18:50.396 --> 01:18:52.810
they work in thousands
to millions.

01:18:52.811 --> 01:18:55.075
Right and they ask for
that kind of scale.

01:18:55.076 --> 01:18:57.635
Not just scale of deployment
but scale of management,

01:18:57.636 --> 01:19:01.045
scale of state management,
scale of monitoring.

01:19:01.046 --> 01:19:03.235
All of that has to be always

01:19:03.236 --> 01:19:05.740
at least a million devices
that you think of.

01:19:05.741 --> 01:19:07.480
So, that's something that we have

01:19:07.481 --> 01:19:09.400
been thinking a lot about.

01:19:09.401 --> 01:19:12.095
We provide zero-touch for
millions of devices today.

01:19:12.096 --> 01:19:13.805
There's a lot to be done there;

01:19:13.806 --> 01:19:15.420
supply chain has to come up,

01:19:15.421 --> 01:19:17.500
silicone has to evolve,

01:19:17.501 --> 01:19:19.670
and all of that is happening.

01:19:19.671 --> 01:19:23.755
Adapting workloads based on
hardware, network, cost,

01:19:23.756 --> 01:19:26.810
so all Edge devices
would not be the same,

01:19:26.811 --> 01:19:29.030
they will be
different small, big.

01:19:29.031 --> 01:19:30.830
So you have to upload,

01:19:30.831 --> 01:19:32.215
you have to have
the right modules

01:19:32.216 --> 01:19:33.820
which go there. How
do you do that?

01:19:33.821 --> 01:19:36.125
There is an optimization
problem in there which

01:19:36.126 --> 01:19:39.130
together with Victor,
Steve, we're investigating.

01:19:39.131 --> 01:19:42.370
There's a lot of work that
we're doing on security.

01:19:42.371 --> 01:19:44.885
When you move Cloud workloads

01:19:44.886 --> 01:19:46.940
to Edge devices
deployed out there,

01:19:46.941 --> 01:19:48.575
you have to be very
careful with that.

01:19:48.576 --> 01:19:50.300
You have to have
the right silicone,

01:19:50.301 --> 01:19:51.835
you have to have
the right models.

01:19:51.836 --> 01:19:54.330
Operations, I talked
about that but one thing

01:19:54.331 --> 01:19:56.830
that has come up again
and again, Edge devices,

01:19:56.831 --> 01:19:58.240
when they are deployed even on

01:19:58.241 --> 01:20:01.185
these mining operations
are not single vendor,

01:20:01.186 --> 01:20:02.520
they're never single vendor.

01:20:02.521 --> 01:20:05.040
There is the owner
of the operation,

01:20:05.041 --> 01:20:06.360
there's the owner of the pump,

01:20:06.361 --> 01:20:08.355
which is different
and it leads to them.

01:20:08.356 --> 01:20:11.530
There is the oil,
the entity which has

01:20:11.531 --> 01:20:15.175
interest in the oil who's
selling that oil to somebody.

01:20:15.176 --> 01:20:17.010
All of them need a slice of

01:20:17.011 --> 01:20:19.920
the data and a slice
of that operation,

01:20:19.921 --> 01:20:22.135
and all of that has to be part

01:20:22.136 --> 01:20:24.255
of our access control models,

01:20:24.256 --> 01:20:25.500
part of how we deploy,

01:20:25.501 --> 01:20:26.970
and the policies and so on.

