FPGA is not the whole thing,

01:38:10.101 --> 01:38:11.765
but part of architecture.

01:38:11.766 --> 01:38:15.260
I think in the 5G infrastructure

01:38:15.261 --> 01:38:16.930
we would try to
software as a codified.

01:38:16.931 --> 01:38:19.060
I think using FPGA,

01:38:19.061 --> 01:38:21.800
you should bring FPGA
as part of architecture

01:38:21.801 --> 01:38:23.065
instead of have everything in

01:38:23.066 --> 01:38:24.810
FPGA is special and how it work.

01:38:24.811 --> 01:38:27.835
I think you should
select the way you

01:38:27.836 --> 01:38:29.630
use them for the critical paths

01:38:29.631 --> 01:38:31.090
for example the data plan.

01:38:31.091 --> 01:38:32.475
&gt;&gt; So, let me, I let you

01:38:32.476 --> 01:38:33.970
speak Kyle and
the rest of you too.

01:38:33.971 --> 01:38:36.870
So, just to sort be
more, even more crisp.

01:38:36.871 --> 01:38:38.510
There's lot of thoughts about

01:38:38.511 --> 01:38:40.045
Machine Learning on the Edge.

01:38:40.046 --> 01:38:42.810
Like there's a debate on how
much you can do in the Edge,

01:38:42.811 --> 01:38:44.785
how much you can do Cloud, you'd

01:38:44.786 --> 01:38:47.110
shrink the models to
put it on the Edge,

01:38:47.111 --> 01:38:48.670
but when you have
specialized hardware

01:38:48.671 --> 01:38:50.235
that tends to open up

01:38:50.236 --> 01:38:52.570
more avenues to do more stuff

01:38:52.571 --> 01:38:54.930
and so that's kind
of what I'm seeing.

01:38:54.931 --> 01:38:58.890
So, for 5G I understand that
but any other thoughts?

01:38:58.891 --> 01:38:59.910
&gt;&gt; Can I take that?

01:38:59.911 --> 01:39:00.790
&gt;&gt; Yeah, please.

01:39:00.791 --> 01:39:03.640
&gt;&gt; So, I think there's
a place for both.

01:39:03.641 --> 01:39:05.440
There's a place for
general purpose

01:39:05.441 --> 01:39:07.850
hardware CPU
based compute as well as

01:39:07.851 --> 01:39:09.460
very specialized hardware like

01:39:09.461 --> 01:39:12.850
GPU acceleration and
FPGA acceleration.

01:39:12.851 --> 01:39:16.540
In the Edge world, again
worth loads tend to be both.

01:39:16.541 --> 01:39:18.580
But for workloads like

01:39:18.581 --> 01:39:20.735
machine learning and
very specialized AI,

01:39:20.736 --> 01:39:24.225
yes FPGA is an avenue,
GPUs are avenue.

01:39:24.226 --> 01:39:26.170
We already have
brainwave which we

01:39:26.171 --> 01:39:28.640
announced a few months back,

01:39:28.641 --> 01:39:32.530
which allows you to
download your ML into

01:39:32.531 --> 01:39:34.400
your hardware which has FPGA and

01:39:34.401 --> 01:39:36.530
then it fully uses the FPGA.

01:39:36.531 --> 01:39:38.525
So, that's brainwave,
it's already announced.

01:39:38.526 --> 01:39:39.730
We also have a bunch of

01:39:39.731 --> 01:39:42.255
collaborations on
cameras with GPUs,

01:39:42.256 --> 01:39:43.995
Qualcomm is one of our partners

01:39:43.996 --> 01:39:46.170
where we enable the GPUs to be

01:39:46.171 --> 01:39:48.100
used on the camera for

01:39:48.101 --> 01:39:50.705
acceleration of
frame detection and so on.

01:39:50.706 --> 01:39:52.930
That's all at the
Edge doing that.

01:39:52.931 --> 01:39:54.675
So, yes, there's
a place for both,

01:39:54.676 --> 01:39:56.865
but I think in the end game,

01:39:56.866 --> 01:39:58.580
it won't be any one of those.

01:39:58.581 --> 01:40:03.895
I think it will always
be a combination to do.

01:40:03.896 --> 01:40:07.380
&gt;&gt; So since my core research is

01:40:07.381 --> 01:40:10.990
really in optimization
algorithms,

01:40:10.991 --> 01:40:13.990
so I hasten to comment
too much on hardware,

01:40:13.991 --> 01:40:17.020
but I just want to quickly
point out two things.

01:40:17.021 --> 01:40:20.685
One is that, I think it was
Ken who asked about security.

01:40:20.686 --> 01:40:24.440
I have this uninformed
unbiased opinion

01:40:24.441 --> 01:40:27.180
that on the Edge of

01:40:27.181 --> 01:40:30.954
the hardware-based
security probably

01:40:30.955 --> 01:40:33.925
isn't much likely to trust.

01:40:33.926 --> 01:40:36.970
Now, as to the second one is that

01:40:36.971 --> 01:40:38.650
Microsoft is moving fast and

01:40:38.651 --> 01:40:40.930
so are some of
the other companies.

01:40:40.931 --> 01:40:44.700
So some of these are not
technologically driven per

01:40:44.701 --> 01:40:46.980
se but really is trying to tip

01:40:46.981 --> 01:40:50.160
the balance of power in
industry food chain.

01:40:50.161 --> 01:40:53.855
Sometimes, having
specialized hardware

01:40:53.856 --> 01:40:55.880
may or may not be
actually of use in

01:40:55.881 --> 01:40:59.070
a particular domain
but a lot of companies

01:40:59.071 --> 01:41:00.660
certainly feel that if I

01:41:00.661 --> 01:41:02.600
don't do something
along those lines,

01:41:02.601 --> 01:41:05.060
I'll be left in the dust as

01:41:05.061 --> 01:41:08.820
the balance of power shifts
with the rise of the Edge.

01:41:08.821 --> 01:41:09.560
&gt;&gt; I see.

01:41:09.561 --> 01:41:11.230
&gt;&gt; A very interesting
observation.

01:41:11.231 --> 01:41:12.380
There's some truth to that.

01:41:12.381 --> 01:41:17.680
&gt;&gt; To add to all of this,
think about wireless networks.

01:41:17.681 --> 01:41:20.300
We're seeing the limiting factor,

01:41:20.301 --> 01:41:23.180
especially with the 5G
technologies,60 gigahertz,

01:41:23.181 --> 01:41:25.510
small cells massive
MIMO especially,

01:41:25.511 --> 01:41:28.640
limiting factor shifting
from what we do in

01:41:28.641 --> 01:41:30.500
the wireless design to

01:41:30.501 --> 01:41:34.285
the computational power behind
the wireless algorithms.

01:41:34.286 --> 01:41:35.760
So computation is becoming

01:41:35.761 --> 01:41:37.990
the bottleneck and
specialized hardware

01:41:37.991 --> 01:41:39.390
is allowing us to be

01:41:39.391 --> 01:41:41.785
more parallel in
the processing that we do

01:41:41.786 --> 01:41:45.845
behind the wireless
algorithms for the Edge.

01:41:45.846 --> 01:41:48.530
So it's going to
play a huge role.

01:41:48.531 --> 01:41:53.595
GPUs and FPGAs in
base stations and on the Edge.

01:41:53.596 --> 01:41:57.810
&gt;&gt; Let me add to this point.

01:41:57.811 --> 01:42:00.045
In our own experience in our lab,

01:42:00.046 --> 01:42:03.270
similar to what you were
saying about the Azure Edge.

01:42:03.271 --> 01:42:06.570
We built a cluster
of Raspberry PIs to

01:42:06.571 --> 01:42:10.290
serve as a Fog node and as
I mentioned in my talk,

01:42:10.291 --> 01:42:12.415
we want to create the ecosystem

01:42:12.416 --> 01:42:13.930
at the Edge, similar
to the Cloud.

01:42:13.931 --> 01:42:17.210
So we brought pub-sub
systems like Kafka and

01:42:17.211 --> 01:42:21.050
storage system like Ascended
to run on the Edge.

01:42:21.051 --> 01:42:23.170
Then we were doing
these camera processing

01:42:23.171 --> 01:42:24.835
that I was talking about and

01:42:24.836 --> 01:42:27.750
the limitation is
how many cameras frames

01:42:27.751 --> 01:42:28.820
per second that you can do,

01:42:28.821 --> 01:42:31.310
even if you have a cluster
of Raspberry PIs.

01:42:31.311 --> 01:42:33.995
We are limited by that.
So at that point,

01:42:33.996 --> 01:42:37.010
you have to include some
additional capabilities like

01:42:37.011 --> 01:42:38.350
Nvidia boards in order to

01:42:38.351 --> 01:42:40.140
do some of the image
processing faster.

01:42:40.141 --> 01:42:42.395
So there is an opportunity
to sort of integrate.

01:42:42.396 --> 01:42:43.800
For the mundane stuff,

01:42:43.801 --> 01:42:47.075
you can use
off-the-shelf components

01:42:47.076 --> 01:42:50.870
but for more
specialized processing,

01:42:50.871 --> 01:42:55.445
you may need to incorporate
additional smarts in it.

01:42:55.446 --> 01:42:57.020
&gt;&gt; So I would add to that.

01:42:57.021 --> 01:42:58.930
I think specialized hardware
is all all about

01:42:58.931 --> 01:43:01.280
nonrecurring engineering
cost, right?

01:43:01.281 --> 01:43:03.535
You need a massive quantity

01:43:03.536 --> 01:43:05.630
of the market to amortize

01:43:05.631 --> 01:43:06.785
a nonrecurring engineering cost.

01:43:06.786 --> 01:43:11.965
You need to be lumber
to amortize that.

01:43:11.966 --> 01:43:15.030
So otherwise, it doesn't make

01:43:15.031 --> 01:43:20.600
sense economically to make
[inaudible] just for machinery.

01:43:20.670 --> 01:43:23.820
&gt;&gt; So your point is that you need

01:43:23.821 --> 01:43:26.940
a lot of Edges for specialized
hardware to take off.

01:43:26.941 --> 01:43:28.555
&gt;&gt; Right. Use the same hardware.

01:43:28.556 --> 01:43:30.950
The same hardware will
require a lot of deployments,

01:43:30.951 --> 01:43:35.750
a lot of devices to use and to
amortize in our [inaudible].

01:43:35.751 --> 01:43:38.440
&gt;&gt; Right, so I would have
a follow up question

01:43:38.441 --> 01:43:39.520
on but I will not.

01:43:39.521 --> 01:43:40.945
The follow up question
will be something like

01:43:40.946 --> 01:43:42.905
what kind of hardware
would you bet on?

01:43:42.906 --> 01:43:44.240
But let's table that because

01:43:44.241 --> 01:43:45.765
I have a bunch of
other things to ask.

01:43:45.766 --> 01:43:52.490
So I want to ask Kishore set
up on another vision, right?

01:43:52.491 --> 01:43:55.670
So he said our vision which

01:43:55.671 --> 01:43:57.425
was about Cloud being

01:43:57.426 --> 01:43:59.680
or Edge being parallel
to the Cloud.

01:43:59.681 --> 01:44:02.470
Many of you actually

01:44:02.471 --> 01:44:04.510
articulated a different vision

01:44:04.511 --> 01:44:06.570
where the Cloud and
Edge play together.

01:44:06.571 --> 01:44:09.225
Cloud is absolutely necessary
and the Edge is necessary.

01:44:09.226 --> 01:44:11.440
So for those of you
who did not play

01:44:11.441 --> 01:44:13.750
and not claim the vision
that [inaudible] did,

01:44:13.751 --> 01:44:15.110
I want to see what your reaction

01:44:15.111 --> 01:44:16.200
was to what he was saying.

01:44:16.201 --> 01:44:18.980
Please.

01:44:18.981 --> 01:44:21.580
&gt;&gt; I like the vision a lot.

01:44:21.581 --> 01:44:27.420
There is a distinction between
the Edge using the Cloud

01:44:27.421 --> 01:44:29.770
versus the Edge becoming

01:44:29.771 --> 01:44:34.500
the Cloud or replacing
some of what Cloud can do.

01:44:35.300 --> 01:44:39.315
I want to highlight
that sometimes,

01:44:39.316 --> 01:44:41.985
that word Cloud can also like

01:44:41.986 --> 01:44:45.245
word Edge and Fog be
used in multiple ways.

01:44:45.246 --> 01:44:46.995
So one particular example is,

01:44:46.996 --> 01:44:49.730
this is regarding
a particular startup that I

01:44:49.731 --> 01:44:52.970
founded called DataMe and
details doesn't matter.

01:44:52.971 --> 01:44:56.090
But it turns out that
when we try to do

01:44:56.091 --> 01:44:59.590
billing and the bite
counting in an LTE network,

01:44:59.591 --> 01:45:01.380
if you do it in the core network,

01:45:01.381 --> 01:45:03.320
which can be drawn
with a big Cloud

01:45:03.321 --> 01:45:06.510
occupying 75 percent
of the slide,

01:45:06.511 --> 01:45:09.100
then A is expensive,

01:45:09.101 --> 01:45:10.500
B is privacy intruding,

01:45:10.501 --> 01:45:14.455
and C is slow like
24 hours later,

01:45:14.456 --> 01:45:16.365
the Internet operator
can tell you something

01:45:16.366 --> 01:45:18.605
about the pricing policies.

01:45:18.606 --> 01:45:20.040
So if you want to
innovate pricing,

01:45:20.041 --> 01:45:22.250
you've got to first
innovate doing

01:45:22.251 --> 01:45:26.600
that in the Edge like mount
the end-user devices.

01:45:26.601 --> 01:45:29.620
Maybe for example, to detect
the peak valley condition of

01:45:29.621 --> 01:45:31.530
LTE cell turns out that you don't

01:45:31.531 --> 01:45:33.705
need to rely on the LTE core,

01:45:33.706 --> 01:45:37.770
you can actually do some
of that on the devices.

01:45:37.771 --> 01:45:40.720
But the point is that

01:45:40.721 --> 01:45:44.490
actually is echoing
Kish respond, right?

01:45:44.491 --> 01:45:49.670
That is an example of the
Edge becoming the Cloud,

01:45:49.671 --> 01:45:51.670
not just using the Cloud.

01:45:51.671 --> 01:45:53.760
However, to do that really

01:45:53.761 --> 01:45:57.120
efficiently you can't
rely 100 percent in

01:45:57.121 --> 01:45:59.880
that startups case on

01:45:59.881 --> 01:46:02.660
all the devices doing
them autonomously.

01:46:02.661 --> 01:46:05.580
So we actually had
a little controller in

01:46:05.581 --> 01:46:09.420
the other kind of Cloud
in the Azure or Amazon,

01:46:09.421 --> 01:46:13.450
if that doesn't trigger
some fire alarm here.

01:46:13.451 --> 01:46:16.935
In that Cloud, it's not
the LTE core Cloud.

01:46:16.936 --> 01:46:18.465
It's a different kind of Cloud

01:46:18.466 --> 01:46:19.920
and put a little provision the

01:46:19.921 --> 01:46:22.300
controller to enhance
the efficiency

01:46:22.301 --> 01:46:24.685
coordinating the mobile devices.

01:46:24.686 --> 01:46:28.530
So we still want that kind
of Cloud even though this is

01:46:28.531 --> 01:46:30.310
literally killing a function

01:46:30.311 --> 01:46:32.390
and replacing it
of another Cloud.

01:46:32.391 --> 01:46:33.850
So this particular example,

01:46:33.851 --> 01:46:36.100
this worked out then we have

01:46:36.101 --> 01:46:37.630
60 million users in the world

01:46:37.631 --> 01:46:39.450
using this product right now,

01:46:39.451 --> 01:46:42.500
but it both echoes
the point that Edge

01:46:42.501 --> 01:46:45.715
can replace Cloud or
be on equal footing,

01:46:45.716 --> 01:46:47.920
but also highlights
the point that it

01:46:47.921 --> 01:46:50.340
still doesn't hurt to
have some form of Cloud.

01:46:50.341 --> 01:46:55.720
&gt;&gt; I would disagree
a little bit there.

01:46:55.721 --> 01:46:58.090
You still need the Cloud.

01:46:58.091 --> 01:46:59.460
I'll tell you what
are the scenarios

01:46:59.461 --> 01:47:00.855
in which you actually need

01:47:00.856 --> 01:47:03.030
a Cloud to bootstrap

01:47:03.031 --> 01:47:05.345
your Edge devices. This
is how we do that.

01:47:05.346 --> 01:47:08.775
One of them is security
or more precisely, trust.

01:47:08.776 --> 01:47:11.600
When you have all of
your devices which now you

01:47:11.601 --> 01:47:14.550
call your Cloud distributed,

01:47:14.551 --> 01:47:17.020
how do you build
trust amongst them?

01:47:17.021 --> 01:47:19.190
Where is your center of trust?

01:47:19.191 --> 01:47:21.330
So Cloud gives you
a convenient way of saying,

01:47:21.331 --> 01:47:23.050
"Okay, trust is in the Cloud."

01:47:23.051 --> 01:47:25.570
From there, I can actually
start trusting software,

01:47:25.571 --> 01:47:26.735
do attestations,

01:47:26.736 --> 01:47:29.780
trust the hardware and then
I can start deploying these.

01:47:29.781 --> 01:47:32.200
So essentially, there's
a master-slave relationship

01:47:32.201 --> 01:47:34.400
in terms of trust
there. That's one.

01:47:34.401 --> 01:47:36.590
Second is availability or

01:47:36.591 --> 01:47:38.475
high availability scenarios which

01:47:38.476 --> 01:47:43.210
require coordination amongst
distributed compute nodes.

01:47:43.211 --> 01:47:45.070
One way, of course, is that each

01:47:45.071 --> 01:47:46.790
one of those nodes
talk to each other,

01:47:46.791 --> 01:47:48.110
do election and so on,

01:47:48.111 --> 01:47:50.075
but then you still
need some way of

01:47:50.076 --> 01:47:53.475
understanding that these nodes
are distributed widely.

01:47:53.476 --> 01:47:54.870
How do you coordinate
between them?

01:47:54.871 --> 01:47:56.980
So you need some place
where they all come down

01:47:56.981 --> 01:47:59.550
to and Cloud would
allow you to do that.

01:47:59.551 --> 01:48:03.240
So, and of course, the final
thing here is how would

01:48:03.241 --> 01:48:07.430
you manage a very large data
set? You could do that.

01:48:07.431 --> 01:48:09.860
You could create little nodes
with Raspberry Pis,

01:48:09.861 --> 01:48:12.640
put little data in all of
them and start coordinating.

01:48:12.641 --> 01:48:14.605
But it's a huge
data problem then.

01:48:14.606 --> 01:48:17.070
Rather than going to the Cloud,

01:48:17.071 --> 01:48:18.250
putting all your data and data

01:48:18.251 --> 01:48:19.810
doing machine learning there

01:48:19.811 --> 01:48:20.980
because you have almost

01:48:20.981 --> 01:48:22.785
infinite compute and
resources there,

01:48:22.786 --> 01:48:25.545
and then moving it influence
down to those Edge nodes.

01:48:25.546 --> 01:48:29.400
So while I do see Edge becoming
more and more powerful,

01:48:29.401 --> 01:48:33.000
Edge supporting
so many more scenarios but I

01:48:33.001 --> 01:48:34.960
still see there's a place for

01:48:34.961 --> 01:48:37.420
the Cloud in there for security.

01:48:37.421 --> 01:48:39.150
&gt;&gt; I'd like to add something that

01:48:39.151 --> 01:48:41.205
has not been said by anybody yet.

01:48:41.206 --> 01:48:44.050
So, if you go to
first principles of Cloud,

01:48:44.051 --> 01:48:48.030
part of it was that I sell shoes,

01:48:48.031 --> 01:48:49.675
I don't want my IT department,

01:48:49.676 --> 01:48:52.710
I wanted somebody else to
take care of the whole thing,

01:48:52.711 --> 01:48:54.485
and I'll just write you a check.

01:48:54.486 --> 01:48:56.600
All your machines,
all the upgrades,

01:48:56.601 --> 01:48:59.120
all the other good stuff has
to happen at somewhere else.

01:48:59.121 --> 01:49:01.420
I don't have to. I can
go and sell shoes.

01:49:01.421 --> 01:49:03.340
That's like I was one of

01:49:03.341 --> 01:49:05.230
the big value
proposition for Cloud.

01:49:05.231 --> 01:49:06.340
You didn't have to
worry about hardware.

01:49:06.341 --> 01:49:07.400
You didn't have to
worry about software,

01:49:07.401 --> 01:49:08.750
you didn't have to
worry about management.

01:49:08.751 --> 01:49:11.060
It was a computing resource
available to you.

01:49:11.061 --> 01:49:13.305
So I think, in the Edge work,

01:49:13.306 --> 01:49:15.190
you're going to need
something like that.

01:49:15.191 --> 01:49:17.385
You can't, I mean, if you build

01:49:17.386 --> 01:49:20.580
a system which is completely
separate from that,

01:49:20.581 --> 01:49:22.680
you actually back to
the old world where you're

01:49:22.681 --> 01:49:25.000
now back to you having
an old IT department,

01:49:25.001 --> 01:49:26.620
back to having all these guys
who are trying to

01:49:26.621 --> 01:49:28.585
deal with the Edge Cloud.

01:49:28.586 --> 01:49:29.155
&gt;&gt; Can I?

01:49:29.156 --> 01:49:29.895
&gt;&gt; Yeah, please.

01:49:29.896 --> 01:49:32.390
&gt;&gt; So I just want
to be sure that I

01:49:32.391 --> 01:49:35.200
wasn't articulating that
we get rid of the Cloud.

01:49:35.201 --> 01:49:38.810
All I was saying was that
we want to put the Edge on

01:49:38.811 --> 01:49:40.690
an equal footing with
the Cloud and there are

01:49:40.691 --> 01:49:43.640
applications in which
that is super important.

01:49:43.641 --> 01:49:45.570
So if you think of
what connected vehicles,

01:49:45.571 --> 01:49:47.940
the latency property
suggests that you

01:49:47.941 --> 01:49:49.360
want these kind of communication

01:49:49.361 --> 01:49:50.720
to happen very quickly
with the Edge.

01:49:50.721 --> 01:49:53.540
On the other hand, if all
